// X64GtRuntime — the hand-assembled GREEN-THREAD backend code that MUST be raw bytes rather than Std ops.
// Everything else in the scheduler is a builder-built `StdModule` (Runtime/GtRuntime.maxon). Standalone
// `FunctionCodeChunk`s — `__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline`, `__gt_guard_tripped`, the stack grower
// `__gt_morestack` and `__gt_stack_relocate` — manipulate rsp/rbp and physical callee-saved registers, rewrite a stack's frame-pointer
// chain, or are entered by a `ret` into a frame no prologue built; plus the INLINE syscall shim
// (`emitGtShimmedIatCall`) that X64Backend wraps around every `iatCall` of a GT module.
// None is expressible in Std (no register/rsp/gs-segment spelling), so all are built by calling X64Backend's
// encoders directly, like the `mrt_*` panic runtime (X64Runtime.maxon).
//
// ⭐ THE TWO CHUNKS TOUCH NO `.data` GLOBAL. The context switch passes the `to` GT through in rdx unchanged, so
// the trampoline reads its own GT from rdx (not from any current-GT word); the strand runner owns
// `M->currentGt` (`GtRuntime.emitGtExecute`). The context switch also repoints the NT_TIB stack bounds on every switch
// (`gs:[0x08]`/`gs:[0x10]`, and the `to`/`from` GTs' saved-bounds fields via their rdx/rcx pointers) — a SEGMENT
// and register-pointer accesses, still no rdata relocation, so both chunks stay pure register/stack/segment
// code exactly like the panic runtime. The INLINE shim, by contrast, DOES reference the
// `__sched_tls_teb_offset` `.data` word — and, through the M it finds there, `currentGt` and `systemStackSP` —
// through the ordinary `encodeLeaRegGlobal` rdata-relocation path the backend already resolves.
//
// CALLING CONVENTION — Maxon's internal ABI (StdToX64Conversion): integer arguments in
// [rcx, rdx, rax, r9, rsi, rdi], primary return in R8; callee-saved registers are the GPRs rbx, r12-r15
// and the XMMs xmm6-15 (RegisterAllocator.calleeSavedMask), plus rsp/rbp (the frame). The context switch
// saves EXACTLY that callee-saved set — a `call __gt_context_switch` is an ordinary call, so from a
// suspending function's view it must preserve precisely what any call preserves, no more (rsi/rdi/rax are
// ARGUMENT registers here, hence caller-saved, hence NOT saved).
//
// ⚠⚠ THE SWITCH IS THE ONE CALL WHOSE CALLEE-SAVED PROMISE IS KEPT BY HAND. Every register-allocated
// function keeps it by construction (its prologue preserves what its coloring used); this chunk has no
// prologue and no coloring, and — unlike any ordinary callee — it runs ANOTHER green thread's arbitrary
// code before control returns. So every register the colorer believes survives a call must be saved here
// EXPLICITLY, and that set is exactly the two ordered lists below.

// The callee-saved registers the context switch saves and restores: the GPRs in push order (pop order is the
// exact reverse), then the XMMs into a `sub rsp` area written with `movsd` — the SAME `calleeSavedOrder` and
// `calleeSavedXmmOrder` the ordinary prologue/epilogue preserves (X64PrologueEpilogue), so the switch
// preserves EXACTLY what any call preserves, from two shared lists rather than second copies. Their sizes are
// the contract `__gt_spawn`'s initial frame layout byte-matches: `GtInitialFrameBytes` = the GPR words + the
// XMM words + the return-address word, and the trampoline address sits in that top word so the first
// switch-in's `ret` enters the trampoline. `assertGtFrameConsistent` (below) enforces that byte-match at
// chunk-build time, deriving it from BOTH lists.
//
// ⚠ ONE `XmmSaveSlotBytes` WORD PER XMM — the SAME constant the prologue's slots stride by, not a second 8
// that happens to agree. The low 64 bits ARE the value in the compiler (no type is wider, and nothing reads bits
// 64-127); the full argument and the condition that would invalidate it live once in `X64PrologueEpilogue`'s
// header, beside that constant. Widen it there and `assertGtFrameConsistent`, which derives from it, panics on
// the next async compile.

// The register the context switch reads `from` (arg 0) and `to` (arg 1) from — the ABI's first two integer
// argument registers. `to` rides through UNMODIFIED so a fresh GT's trampoline reads its GT from rdx.
let GtSwitchFromReg = X64Register.rcx
let GtSwitchToReg = X64Register.rdx

// The scratch the context switch shuttles the NT_TIB stack bounds through (save `from`'s, load `to`'s). rax is
// caller-saved and is not read across a switch by any resumed continuation, so clobbering it here is invisible.
let GtSwitchTibScratchReg = X64Register.rax

// NT_TIB stack-bound fields in the GS segment (Win64). A kernel call probes these and faults when RSP is
// outside `[StackLimit, StackBase)`. The syscall shim (repoint at the system stack for a call) and the context
// switch (repoint at the resumed GT's stack) write them and the backtrace walker reads them, so they are named
// once here.
module let TibStackBaseDisp = 0x08 as Imm32  // NT_TIB.StackBase  — gs:[0x08]
module let TibStackLimitDisp = 0x10 as Imm32  // NT_TIB.StackLimit — gs:[0x10]

// The NT_TIB stack bounds are a PAIR (base, limit); these two helpers spell that two-word structure ONCE for
// the three sites that move it — the context switch's save + load, and the shim's after-call restore (which is
// exactly a load). `scratch` shuttles each word (`gtReg` and `scratch` must differ); neither helper touches any
// other register.
function emitSaveTibIntoGt(code ByteArray, gtReg X64Register, scratch X64Register)
	encodeMovRegGsAbs(code, dest: scratch, disp: TibStackBaseDisp)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: gtReg, disp: GtOffTibStackBase as Imm32, srcReg: scratch)
	encodeMovRegGsAbs(code, dest: scratch, disp: TibStackLimitDisp)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: gtReg, disp: GtOffTibStackLimit as Imm32, srcReg: scratch)
end 'emitSaveTibIntoGt'

function emitLoadTibFromGt(code ByteArray, gtReg X64Register, scratch X64Register)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: scratch, base: gtReg, disp: GtOffTibStackBase as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeMovGsAbsReg(code, disp: TibStackBaseDisp, srcReg: scratch)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: scratch, base: gtReg, disp: GtOffTibStackLimit as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeMovGsAbsReg(code, disp: TibStackLimitDisp, srcReg: scratch)
end 'emitLoadTibFromGt'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **DOES THIS OS PROBE A PER-THREAD STACK-BOUNDS PAIR ON A KERNEL CALL?** Windows does: `NT_TIB`'s
// `StackBase`/`StackLimit` are read by the kernel on entry and a call made with RSP outside them FAULTS, which
// is the whole reason this file moves a pair of words on every context switch, every syscall shim and every
// stack grow — and why the backtrace walker can read the running stack's bounds there whatever stack it is.
// Linux does not — the kernel reads the pointer it is given and nothing else — so on that lane every one of
// those sites emits NOTHING here, and the GT struct's two bounds fields are never read.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE OS's QUESTION AND NOT THE ROUTE'S, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT `x64OsCallKind`.** That one asks how
// this backend reaches the kernel (an import or a `syscall`); this one asks what the kernel demands of the
// stack once it is there, and the two are independent facts that happen to split the same two OSes today.
module function x64OsProbesStackBounds(os Os) returns bool
	return match os 'os'
		windows gives true
		linux gives false
		macos panic("x64OsProbesStackBounds: there is no x64-macOS backend — `buildBackend` dispatches macOS to the arm64 backend, which probes no bounds pair either")
		wasi panic("x64OsProbesStackBounds: wasm32-wasi is not an x64 target — a WASI component has no OS-thread stack for a kernel to probe")
	end 'os'
end 'x64OsProbesStackBounds'

// ⭐ **`__gt_morestack`'s PAGE SOURCE (step 5) — `rax = <new_size bytes>`, with `new_size` in r14.** The two
// OSes agree on nothing here but the answer: Windows commits and reserves in one call, Linux maps anonymous
// private pages the first touch faults in. Both leave the base in rax, which is what lets step 5 read one
// register whichever lane it is on.
function emitGtMorestackAllocate(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, os Os)
	match x64OsCallKind(os) 'route'
		importCall then emitGtMorestackVirtualAlloc(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs)
		syscall then emitGtMorestackMmap(code)
	end 'route'
end 'emitGtMorestackAllocate'

// `VirtualAlloc(NULL, new_size, MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_READWRITE)`.
function emitGtMorestackVirtualAlloc(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rcx, wide: true)  // lpAddress = NULL
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: GtMoreNewSizeReg, wide: true)  // dwSize = new_size
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.r8, value: VirtualAllocCommitAndReserve)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.r9, value: PageReadWriteProtection)
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualAlloc))
end 'emitGtMorestackVirtualAlloc'

function emitGtMorestackMmap(code ByteArray)
	encodeX64MmapAnon(code, length: X64MapLength.inRegister(GtMoreNewSizeReg))
end 'emitGtMorestackMmap'

// ⭐ **`__gt_stack_relocate`'s RELEASE — give back the old stack, whose base is in r12 and whose length is in
// r13.** ⚠ **WINDOWS FORBIDS THE LENGTH AND POSIX REQUIRES IT**, which is `lowerOsFreePages`' split exactly:
// `MEM_RELEASE` names a whole reservation by its base alone, while `munmap` names a range. Neither answer is
// captured — a failed release leaves the routine nothing to do, and every pointer into the old stack has
// already been rewritten.
function emitGtStackRelease(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, os Os)
	match x64OsCallKind(os) 'route'
		importCall then emitGtStackVirtualFree(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs)
		syscall then emitGtStackMunmap(code)
	end 'route'
end 'emitGtStackRelease'

function emitGtStackVirtualFree(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: RelocOldBaseReg, wide: true)  // lpAddress = old_base
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: VirtualFreeSizeMustBeZero)  // dwSize = 0
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.r8, value: VirtualFreeMemRelease)  // dwFreeType = MEM_RELEASE
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualFree))
end 'emitGtStackVirtualFree'

function emitGtStackMunmap(code ByteArray)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdi, src: RelocOldBaseReg, wide: true)
	encodeX64MunmapBaseInRdi(code, length: X64MapLength.inRegister(RelocOldSizeReg))
end 'emitGtStackMunmap'

// Repoint the NT_TIB at the single SYSTEM stack `[topReg - GtSystemStackSize, topReg)` — `gs:[0x08] = top`,
// `gs:[0x10] = top - size`. The two sites that switch a kernel call onto the system stack — the syscall shim
// (before an `iatCall`) and `__gt_morestack` (step 3, for its VirtualAlloc and the relocation's VirtualFree) —
// compute those TWO TIB words the SAME way, so the `top - size` structure is spelled ONCE here rather than open-coded twice,
// where an edit to one could silently repoint one stack differently from the other. `topReg` is DESTROYED (it
// becomes `top - size`); both callers hold `top` in a register they are otherwise done with.
function emitRepointTibToSystemStack(code ByteArray, topReg X64Register)
	encodeMovGsAbsReg(code, disp: TibStackBaseDisp, srcReg: topReg)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: topReg, imm: GtSystemStackSize as Imm32)
	encodeMovGsAbsReg(code, disp: TibStackLimitDisp, srcReg: topReg)
end 'emitRepointTibToSystemStack'

// The scratch registers the trampoline holds across the straight-line body: r12 = the GT (callee-saved, so it
// survives the user call), r10 = the argument buffer, r11 = the function pointer. None is an argument register,
// so the six arg-register loads below cannot clobber them.
let GtTrampGtReg = X64Register.r12
let GtTrampArgBufReg = X64Register.r10
let GtTrampFuncPtrReg = X64Register.r11

// The bytes the context switch reserves below its GPR pushes for the callee-saved XMM half — one
// `XmmSaveSlotBytes` slot per register of `calleeSavedXmmOrder`, which IS the width the prologue's slots use
// because it is the same constant.
function gtXmmSaveAreaBytes() returns StackSize
	return calleeSavedXmmOrder.count() * XmmSaveSlotBytes
end 'gtXmmSaveAreaBytes'

// ⭐ Enforce the ONE fact the Std-tier frame builder (`__gt_spawn` in GtRuntime, which lays `GtInitialFrameBytes`)
// and this Target-tier byte code SHARE: a fresh GT's initial context-switch frame is every callee-saved
// register plus one return-address word. If a register is added to or removed from EITHER callee-saved list
// without moving `GtInitialFrameBytes` in lockstep, the first switch-in pops the wrong number of bytes and
// `ret`s into a WILD address. This turns that silent cross-tier drift into a compile-time panic on every
// async compile.
//
// ⚠⚠ IT IS DERIVED FROM **BOTH** LISTS. The GPRs are pushed words; the XMMs are `movsd` words in a `sub rsp`
// area, because x86-64 has no `push xmm`. An assert over `calleeSavedOrder` alone would keep passing while
// covering only half the frame.
//
// ⚠⚠ AND FROM THE PROLOGUE'S OWN STRIDE (`XmmSaveSlotBytes`, via `gtXmmSaveAreaBytes`), not from any other
// constant that happens to be 8: widening the prologue's slots to 16 must fail here, or the context switch
// would save 8 of every 16 bytes and destroy the upper halves of xmm6-15 across every `await`.
function assertGtFrameConsistent(target Target)
	let gprBytes = calleeSavedOrder.count() * StackWordBytes
	let framedBytes = gprBytes + gtXmmSaveAreaBytes() + StackWordBytes

	let declared = gtInitialFrameBytes(target) as StackSize

	if framedBytes != declared 'drift'
		panic("GT frame drift: the context switch saves {calleeSavedOrder.count()} callee-saved GPR words ({gprBytes} bytes) + {calleeSavedXmmOrder.count()} callee-saved XMM words ({gtXmmSaveAreaBytes()} bytes) + 1 return-address word = {framedBytes} bytes, but `gtInitialFrameBytes` (the frame __gt_spawn lays for this target) is {declared} — a mismatch is a wild ret on the first switch-in")
	end 'drift'
end 'assertGtFrameConsistent'

// `__gt_context_switch(from = rcx, to = rdx)` — save the current GT's callee-saved register + rsp/rbp state
// into `from`, restore `to`'s, and `ret` into `to`'s saved return address (a resumed GT's await-loop
// continuation, or — for a fresh GT — the trampoline). No frame, no `.data`.
function buildGtContextSwitchChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	assertGtFrameConsistent(target)
	var code = ByteArray.create()

	// Save callee-saved on the current (from) stack, in push order.
	for reg in calleeSavedOrder 'push'
		encodePushReg(code, reg: reg)
	end 'push'

	// ⭐⭐ AND THE CALLEE-SAVED XMMs — `sub rsp` + `movsd`, because there is no `push xmm`.
	//
	// THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT SYMMETRY FOR ITS OWN SAKE. Ordinary user code reaches this chunk
	// through a normal `call` (`__gt_await` → `__gt_context_switch`), so the colorer treats it as a call
	// clobbering exactly `callerSavedMask()` — which EXCLUDES xmm6-15. The colorer therefore believes a float
	// in xmm6 survives this call. But this chunk does not return to its caller: it switches to a DIFFERENT
	// green thread's stack, that thread runs arbitrary user code, and control comes back here only later.
	// Without these saves the other thread's float work lands in xmm6-15 and the suspended thread resumes
	// reading it — a silent wrong answer with no crash to find it by.
	//
	// One slot per callee-saved XMM — bounded by that list, two orders of magnitude below the guard
	// page, so this reservation never walks and needs no scratch (see `encodePrologue`).
	encodePrologue(code, stackSize: gtXmmSaveAreaBytes(), probeScratchIsFree: false)

	for k in 0 upto calleeSavedXmmOrder.count() 'saveXmm'
		let reg = try calleeSavedXmmOrder.get(k) otherwise panic("buildGtContextSwitchChunk: save index derived from count is in range")
		encodeMovsdBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: (k * XmmSaveSlotBytes) as Imm32, srcReg: reg)
	end 'saveXmm'

	// from->sp = rsp ; from->fp = rbp.
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtSwitchFromReg, disp: GtOffSp as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rsp)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtSwitchFromReg, disp: GtOffFp as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rbp)

	// ⭐ SAVE the CURRENT NT_TIB stack bounds into `from`, so the TIB always describes the stack that is actually
	// executing. Without this, the syscall shim's after-call restore (which sets the TIB to `currentGt`'s bounds)
	// would leave the TIB describing the OUTGOING GT's stack after a switch, and the RESUMED GT's next kernel call
	// would probe a TIB that does not contain its RSP and fault. This is also what SEEDS an M's scheduler
	// context's bounds: it never runs the shim (its `stackBase == 0`), so the first switch AWAY from it is the
	// only place its OS thread's real TIB bounds are captured. Uses rax as scratch — caller-saved and not read
	// across a switch.
	if x64OsProbesStackBounds(target.os) 'saveTheBounds'
		emitSaveTibIntoGt(code, gtReg: GtSwitchFromReg, scratch: GtSwitchTibScratchReg)
	end 'saveTheBounds'

	// rsp = to->sp ; rbp = to->fp — now on the destination stack.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rsp, base: GtSwitchToReg, disp: GtOffSp as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rbp, base: GtSwitchToReg, disp: GtOffFp as Imm32, wide: true)

	// LOAD the resumed GT's saved TIB bounds into the NT_TIB, matching the stack we just switched to (`__gt_spawn`
	// seeds a fresh GT's bounds; the save above keeps every GT's fresh). The pair keeps `gs:[0x08]`/`gs:[0x10]`
	// consistent with RSP across every switch.
	if x64OsProbesStackBounds(target.os) 'loadTheBounds'
		emitLoadTibFromGt(code, gtReg: GtSwitchToReg, scratch: GtSwitchTibScratchReg)
	end 'loadTheBounds'

	// Restore the callee-saved XMMs from the new stack FIRST — they sit BELOW the pushed GPR words, so rsp
	// addresses them before the `add rsp` that releases their area. Restore order is free (each register has
	// its own word rather than a stack position); only the GPR pops below are LIFO.
	for k in 0 upto calleeSavedXmmOrder.count() 'restoreXmm'
		let reg = try calleeSavedXmmOrder.get(k) otherwise panic("buildGtContextSwitchChunk: restore index derived from count is in range")
		encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: reg, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: (k * XmmSaveSlotBytes) as Imm32)
	end 'restoreXmm'

	encodeEpilogue(code, stackSize: gtXmmSaveAreaBytes())

	// Restore callee-saved from the new stack, in reverse push order, then `ret` into to's saved return addr.
	for i in 0 upto calleeSavedOrder.count() 'pop'
		let reg = try calleeSavedOrder.get(calleeSavedOrder.count() - 1 - i) otherwise panic("buildGtContextSwitchChunk: pop index derived from count is in range")
		encodePopReg(code, reg: reg)
	end 'pop'

	code.push(OpcodeRet)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(GtContextSwitchName, code: code, callFixups: CallFixupArray.create(), rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(), iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(), rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtContextSwitchChunk'

// `__gt_trampoline` — the entry a FRESH green thread `ret`s into on its first switch-in. NOT a normal call:
// no incoming register arguments and no prologue built for it. It reads its own GT from rdx (which the
// context switch left holding `to`), loads the async function + arg buffer from the GT, loads all
// MaxAsyncArgs argument slots into the ABI argument registers, calls the function, captures its R8 result
// and marks the GT completed, then context-switches to the GT's waiter (always set by the strand runner that
// switched it in).
//
// The stack is 16-aligned on entry (`__gt_spawn` lays `gt.sp` so the switch-in `ret` lands with rsp = stack
// top, a page boundary), so `sub rsp, ShadowSpaceBytes` — a multiple of 16 — keeps every `call` below aligned
// while reserving the Win64 shadow space the user call may clobber.
function buildGtTrampolineChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()

	// gt = rdx (the context switch's `to`, unmodified). Held in a callee-saved register across the user call.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtTrampGtReg, src: GtSwitchToReg, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: GtTrampArgBufReg, base: GtTrampGtReg, disp: GtOffArgBuf as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: GtTrampFuncPtrReg, base: GtTrampGtReg, disp: GtOffFuncPtr as Imm32, wide: true)

	// Reserve the user call's shadow space (see the alignment note above). 32 bytes, and r11 is
	// LIVE across it (it carries the callee's function pointer, called below) — so this must never
	// be the reservation that walks, and `probeScratchIsFree: false` is what says so.
	encodePrologue(code, stackSize: ShadowSpaceBytes, probeScratchIsFree: false)

	// Load every argument slot into its ABI argument register. The callee reads only the slots it declared;
	// the rest are the arg buffer's slab zeros in unread registers. `argRegisterAt` is the ABI's own
	// arg-register oracle, so this load order cannot drift from a call site's arg order.
	for k in 0 upto MaxAsyncArgs 'args'
		encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: argRegisterAt(k as ParamCount), base: GtTrampArgBufReg, disp: (k * GtArgSlotBytes) as Imm32, wide: true)
	end 'args'

	// Call the async function; capture its R8 result AND its R10 error flag into the GT; mark completed.
	// R8 (result) and R10 (the dual-register error flag) are the throws ABI's two return registers and are
	// NOT argument registers, so both survive the arg-register loads above and hold the thunk's return values
	// here. R10 is captured UNCONDITIONALLY: for a NON-throwing thunk it is garbage, but harmless — only
	// `__gt_try_await` ever reads `gt.threw`, and a non-throwing promise is awaited via `__gt_await`, which
	// does not. This is the same r10 the trampoline used as its arg-buffer scratch (`GtTrampArgBufReg`) before
	// the call; that use is finished once the args are loaded, so the register is free to receive the flag.
	encodeCallReg(code, reg: GtTrampFuncPtrReg)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtTrampGtReg, disp: GtOffResult as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.r8)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtTrampGtReg, disp: GtOffThrew as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.r10)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: GtStatusCompleted)

	// ⭐⭐ **THIS `mov` IS THE PUBLICATION, AND ON x64 IT IS ALREADY A STORE-RELEASE (G3).** A reader on
	// another M — an awaiter's registration, `__gt_is_complete` — tests `gt.status == completed` and then reads
	// the two words stored just above, so the flag must not become visible before them. x86-64 is TOTAL STORE
	// ORDER — stores are never reordered with other stores — so an ordinary `mov` gives exactly that, and
	// nothing extra is emitted here. (`mfence` would order STORE→LOAD, a sequential-consistency obligation
	// this publication does not have.) The weakly-ordered lanes are where the instruction has to change:
	// `Arm64GtRuntime.buildArm64GtTrampolineChunk` emits an `STLR` at this exact point and says so.
	//
	// ⚠ There is no COMPILER half to worry about either, unlike the Std-tier publication
	// (`GtRuntime.emitGtPublishCompleted`): these are hand-assembled bytes, so no pass can reorder them.
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtTrampGtReg, disp: GtOffStatus as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// context_switch(from = gt, to = gt.waiter) — never returns (the GT is completed).
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: GtSwitchToReg, base: GtTrampGtReg, disp: GtOffWaiter as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtSwitchFromReg, src: GtTrampGtReg, wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtContextSwitchName)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(GtTrampolineName, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(), iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(), rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtTrampolineChunk'

// --- The Win32 syscall shim (P1.5-B1a′ slice 1) ---
//
// A green thread's kernel call runs on its M's 64 KB SYSTEM stack, not the GT's own small stack: heavy
// Win32 calls (CreateProcessA, WriteFile) use deep stack and probe the Windows TIB, and would overrun a
// green thread's seed stack, which nothing grows for a frame the prologue guard never sees. Emitted inline
// around EVERY `iatCall` of a GT module (the runtime gate makes it a no-op off a GT stack), so a program that
// never touches the scheduler is unchanged.
//
// ⭐ WHY IT COPIES THE STACK ARGS instead of writing them AFTER the switch. The runtimes here are
// builder-built Std, REGISTER-ALLOCATED, and both spills and outgoing args are addressed
// RSP-relative (X64Backend spills; `win64OutgoingArgDisp`). Switching RSP before the arg-setup ops
// would make any spill RELOAD the allocator inserts for an arg operand read the SYSTEM stack — a
// silent miscompile. So the shim wraps ONLY the `iatCall` with RSP unchanged through all arg-setup
// (spills/arg-stores land on the GT frame at `[rsp+0x20+k*8]`), then COPIES the N outgoing
// stack-arg words onto the system stack after the switch. RSP moves only
// inside the shim's own bytes and is restored before the next TargetOp — invisible to the register allocator.
//
// ⭐ REGISTER DISCIPLINE. Win64 passes integer args in rcx/rdx/r8/r9 and returns in rax; the shim preserves
// those. It uses ONLY rax/rcx/r10/r11 as scratch — every one caller-saved and in `iatCall`'s `implicitDefs`
// (RegisterAllocator.callerSavedMask), so the allocator already treats them as call-destroyed and the shim's
// use is invisible. The args (rcx/rdx/r8/r9) are untouched BEFORE the call; the result (rax) is untouched
// AFTER it — the post-call TIB restore uses rcx, which is dead once the call has returned.
//
// ⚠ ONE REGION PER MACHINE, AND THE SAFETY IS PER OS THREAD — NOT PER PROCESSOR, AND NOT "ONE AT A TIME".
// The shim switches to THIS M's FIXED `systemStackSP` (`SchedRuntime.MOffSystemStackSP`, reset every entry,
// not a bump pointer). Several Ms are routinely inside shimmed calls at once, and that is fine BY
// CONSTRUCTION: they are different regions. What must never happen is one OS THREAD re-entering its own
// region while a frame is live in it. The region is the M's and not the P's because `handoffp` can take an
// M's P while it is inside a kernel call. `multicore-stress/syscall-stack-torture.maxon` pins it.
//
// ⭐ RE-ENTRY DOES HAPPEN NOW, AND IT IS SAFE FOR A STATED REASON RATHER THAN BY ABSENCE. A kernel call is a
// LEAF that never re-enters guarded Maxon code, so the call itself cannot nest — but `exitsyscall`'s slow
// path calls `__sched_syscall_regain`, which takes `__sched_lock` through a shimmed `EnterCriticalSection`.
// That is a second entry on the same thread, and it is safe because the slow path runs on the GT STACK: the
// unwind above has already restored RSP off the system stack, so the inner entry re-reads `systemStackSP`
// with nothing of the outer call live above it.

// A pushed 64-bit register is 8 bytes. The shim pushes two such words (the caller's r10 on the GT stack, then
// the saved GT rsp on the system stack). This is BOTH the offset from `r10` — which points at the first pushed
// word after `push r10 ; mov r10, rsp` — up to the original RSP where the GT-frame stack args sit, AND the pad
// the reserved system-stack frame adds so the second push leaves RSP 16-aligned at the `call`.
// It IS `StackWordBytes` — "what a `push r64` moves rsp by" is that constant's own definition — and says so
// rather than repeating an 8, for the reason `XmmSaveSlotBytes` does (X64PrologueEpilogue).
let ShimPushedWordBytes = StackWordBytes as StackSize

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_shim_enter` — THE SHIM'S FIRST INSTRUCTION, AND A CHUNK RATHER THAN TWO INLINE ONES.** See
// `buildGtShimEnterChunk`: a chunk is never an asynchronous safe point, which is the whole of why the
// machine it answers cannot be one this thread has stopped belonging to.
let GtShimEnterName = b"__gt_shim_enter"

// The shim's scratch registers (see the header's register discipline). None is a Win64 argument register.
let ShimGtReg = X64Register.r11  // currentGt in the guard, then the stack-arg copy shuttle, then reloaded post-call
let ShimGtRspReg = X64Register.r10  // the GT's RSP across the switch (push/pop-balanced)
let ShimTibScratchReg = X64Register.rcx  // post-call TIB-restore scratch; dead after the call, so rax (the result) survives

// The shim's own forward-branch targets, resolved by `resolveChunkRel32Fixups` against a shim-LOCAL fixup list
// and offset map — the same machinery the panic runtime's assembler uses, scoped here so the ids are looked up
// only in the shim's own map and never collide with the enclosing function's block ids.
let ShimDirectLabel = 0 as BlockId
let ShimDoneLabel = 1 as BlockId

// ⭐⭐ **`mDest` = THE M THIS OS THREAD IS, BRANCHING TO `bail` IF THERE ISN'T ONE — the hand-assembled twin
// of `SchedRuntime.emitLoadCurrentM`, and the ONE spelling every Target-tier reader shares.** The syscall
// shim, the prologue stack guard and the backtrace walker (`X64Runtime.emitWalkStackBounds`) each need the
// running green thread, `M->currentGt`; writing the sequence twice is exactly how two of them would come to
// disagree about which of its null cases to test.
//
// ⭐ **NO READER WANTS THE P**: `currentGt` and the syscall stack are both the M's, so the hot path is three
// loads and three tests with no indirection through a P.
//
// **THE TWO NULLS ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND BOTH MUST BE ASKED:**
//   * a ZERO TEB OFFSET means `__sched_init_procs` has not run — the scheduler does not exist yet, and
//     `gs:[0]` is `NT_TIB.ExceptionList`, a NON-NULL pointer, so an unchecked read hands back garbage and the
//     next field load faults. This is reachable: the prologue guard runs inside `main` before the first
//     `async`.
//   * a ZERO M means this OS thread is not one. That is reachable too, by a thread that runs real code: the
//     IOCP completion thread is created by `__io_init`, never becomes an M, and calls builder-built
//     `__gt_enqueue` — whose lock take is a shimmed `iatCall`.
//
// **THE THIRD IS THE GREEN THREAD ITSELF**, and it is inside this helper rather than at each caller for the
// same reason the other two are: every caller asks it and bails on it, and the version where one of them
// forgot would be a load through a zeroed `currentGt`.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `P == 0` TEST HERE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**
// An M may hold no P, but no reader here ever asks for one — so the arm has nothing to guard. The Std-tier
// readers that DO want one test it (`__sched_runq_put`, `emitLoadCurrentGtAndProcIdOrZero`,
// `emitSlabCurrentP`).
//
// `mDest` keeps the M — the shim reads its syscall stack out of it after the branch, so the two registers are
// separate; the guard and the walker pass the same register for both, which is exactly what "the M is dead
// after this" looks like. All three bails go to the caller's own label, which in each caller means "not on
// a green thread".
//
// ⚠ **THE rbp/r13 ENCODING TRAP IS CLOSED BY THE ENCODER, NOT BY A REGISTER CHOICE HERE.** `[rbp]`/`[r13]`
// with mod=00 means RIP-RELATIVE, so a hand-assembled ModR/M would read a constant out of the code stream
// instead of the thread's slot. `encodeMovRegSegBase` routes through the shared
// `encodeModRmBaseDisp`, which emits the zero-`disp8` form for exactly those two bases, so either
// register may be anything.
module function emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, mDest X64Register, gtDest X64Register, bail BlockId, os Os)
	emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, fixups: fixups, dest: mDest, bail: bail, os: os)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: gtDest, base: mDest, disp: MOffCurrentGt as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: gtDest, src: gtDest, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: bail)
end 'emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail'

// The M alone with both of ITS nulls tested — split out because the shim wants the M AFTER it has the GT and
// the two answers cannot share a register.
function emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, dest X64Register, bail BlockId, os Os)
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: dest, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: dest, base: dest, disp: 0, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: dest, src: dest, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: bail)
	encodeMovRegSegBase(code, dest: dest, base: dest, segment: x64ThreadPointerSegment(os))
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: dest, src: dest, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: bail)
end 'emitLoadCurrentMOrBail'

// ⭐⭐ **THE arm64-LINUX TWIN HAS A FOURTH NULL TEST HERE AND THIS LANE DELIBERATELY HAS NONE — the
// difference is that x86-64 CANNOT READ ITS THREAD POINTER AS A VALUE.** `TPIDR_EL0` is an ordinary system
// register, so the other lane spends one free `cbz` on "does this thread have a slot block?". Here FS base is
// not addressable in user mode at all: the only reads are `arch_prctl(ARCH_GET_FS)` — a SYSCALL, which is
// unthinkable in a prologue guard — or a load THROUGH the segment, which is the wild load the test exists to
// prevent.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS ALSO UNREACHABLE, WHICH IS WHY THE ABSENCE IS AN ARGUMENT AND NOT A GAP.** Only two kinds of OS
// thread run Maxon code on this lane and both have a block:
//   * the MAIN thread, which gets one from `mrt_host_tls_alloc` inside `__sched_init_procs` — the same
//     bring-up that publishes the offset word the FIRST test above reads, so "before the block exists" and
//     "before the offset exists" are the same instant and that test already covers it;
//   * a WORKER M, which gets one through `CLONE_SETTLS` before it executes an instruction.
// There is no third: `ioCompletionShape(linux)` is `inlineCompletion`, so this lane starts no IOCP
// completion-drain thread — which is exactly the thread the Windows arm's second null test exists for.
//
// ⚠ **AND IF THAT ARGUMENT WERE EVER FALSIFIED, THE FAILURE IS DIAGNOSABLE RATHER THAN SILENT.** A slot read
// with FS base 0 is a load at `key * 8` — inside the unmapped null page — so it faults at the instruction
// that did it, exactly as the guard page under a worker M's stack does. The other lane buys a named bail for
// one instruction; this one would have to buy it with a syscall, and gets a SIGSEGV for free.

// ⭐ The M alone, where the caller has already PROVEN it exists and therefore has no branch to take.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A WEAKER VERSION OF THE ONE ABOVE, IT IS A DIFFERENT PRECONDITION, AND EACH OF ITS TWO
// CALLERS STATES ITS OWN PROOF.** The shim's post-call TIB restore and the prologue guard's cold path both run
// only after the guarded read above has already answered non-null, and neither can have stopped being an M
// since: a thread becomes an M once (`emitSchedBecomeM`) and never unbecomes one. A test here would need a
// branch target that has no correct destination.
function emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, dest X64Register, os Os)
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: dest, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: dest, base: dest, disp: 0, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegSegBase(code, dest: dest, base: dest, segment: x64ThreadPointerSegment(os))
end 'emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless'

// The count of true Win64 stack arguments (args 5+) a shimmed import takes — the words the shim copies from the
// GT frame onto the system stack. Read from the ISEL's `*StackArgWords` single source (StdToX64Conversion), the
// SAME constant that sizes each call's outgoing-arg reservation — so the copy count is DERIVED from the arg
// layout it must match, never a second copy that could drift into a silent CreateProcessA/WriteFile miscompile
// (too few words copied ⇒ the kernel reads garbage stack args). A ≤4-arg (register-only) import passes nothing
// on the stack and copies nothing.
// ⭐⭐ **THE SHIM'S PER-IMPORT COPY COUNT — TOTAL OVER `IatSlot`, SO AN OMISSION IS A COMPILE ERROR.**
//
// The table must be COMPLETE, and a missing entry's answer — zero words copied — is indistinguishable from a
// correct one: a `__ManagedFile.open*` reached from a SPAWNED green thread would hand the kernel whatever
// happened to be on the system stack for `CreateFileA`'s last three arguments, and only off the main thread,
// where a green suite does not look. So the `match` below is EXHAUSTIVE over the enum, and adding an
// `IatSlot` does not compile until its count is stated. There is no `default` arm and there must never be
// one — a `default gives 0` would be that defect wearing a `match`'s clothes.
//
// ⚠ **A `0` HERE IS A CLAIM, NOT A BLANK.** It says the import passes four or fewer arguments, so Win64
// puts every one in rcx/rdx/r8/r9 and there is nothing on the stack to copy. Each such arm carries its
// argument count so the claim can be checked against MSDN without reading the emitter.
//
// ⚠ **THE EIGHT NON-ZERO COUNTS ARE NOT SPELLED HERE.** They are the SAME constants
// `StdToX64Conversion.stdOpOutgoingArgBytes` reserves the GT frame with, so the copy and the reservation
// cannot disagree about how many words the kernel reads — the property those constants' own headers exist
// to hold. This function maps slots to them; it does not restate them.
// ⚠ **THE SCAN IS DONE ONCE PER SHIM AND ITS ANSWER FEEDS BOTH TABLES** — the copy count and the syscall
// classification below — so the two can never be asked about different slots.
function iatSlotOfIndex(slotIndex RelocSlotIndex) returns IatSlot
	for slot in IatSlot.allCases 'each'
		if iatSlotIndex(slot) == slotIndex 'found'
			return slot
		end 'found'
	end 'each'

	panic("iatSlotOfIndex: slot index {slotIndex} names no IatSlot — every `iatCall` relocation is minted from `iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.…)`, so an index outside the enum means the op carried a hand-built number")
end 'iatSlotOfIndex'

// The stack-argument word count of ONE import, exhaustive over `IatSlot`. See the header above `iatSlotOfIndex`.
function gtShimStackArgWordsOf(slot IatSlot) returns StackSize
	return match slot 'k'
		// --- the imports the kernel reads stack arguments for: the counts live with the arg layout ---
		CreateProcessA gives CreateProcessStackArgWords
		WriteFile gives WriteFileStackArgWords
		CreateNamedPipeW gives CreateNamedPipeStackArgWords
		CreateFileW gives CreateFileStackArgWords

		// The `A` variant takes the SAME seven arguments as its `W` sibling, so it derives from the same
		// constant.
		CreateFileA gives CreateFileStackArgWords
		CreateThread gives CreateThreadStackArgWords
		ReadFile gives ReadFileStackArgWords

		// O3. FIVE arguments, so one word on the stack — derived from the same constant
		// `stdOpOutgoingArgBytes` reserves the frame with, never restated here.
		MapViewOfFile gives MapViewOfFileStackArgWords

		// ⚠ FIVE arguments, so ONE lands on the stack — and this arm is why the enum is worth being exhaustive
		// over. It sat in the old `return 0` fallthrough, where the answer was WRONG but harmless because the
		// completion loop is hand-assembled and calls it DIRECTLY, never through the shim. Stated at its true
		// ABI count rather than at the 0 its current unreachability would excuse, so the answer stays right if
		// it is ever emitted as an `iatCall`.
		GetQueuedCompletionStatus gives GetQueuedCompletionStatusStackArgWords

		// --- four arguments or fewer: register-only, nothing on the stack ---
		ExitProcess gives 0  // 1: uExitCode
		GetStdHandle gives 0  // 1: nStdHandle
		AddVectoredExceptionHandler gives 0  // 2: First, Handler
		VirtualAlloc gives 0  // 4: lpAddress, dwSize, flAllocationType, flProtect
		QueryPerformanceCounter gives 0  // 1: lpPerformanceCount
		QueryPerformanceFrequency gives 0  // 1: lpFrequency
		GetExitCodeProcess gives 0  // 2: hProcess, lpExitCode
		WaitForSingleObject gives 0  // 2: hHandle, dwMilliseconds
		WaitForMultipleObjects gives 0  // 4: nCount, lpHandles, bWaitAll, dwMilliseconds
		CloseHandle gives 0  // 1: hObject
		VirtualFree gives 0  // 3: lpAddress, dwSize, dwFreeType
		SetHandleInformation gives 0  // 3: hObject, dwMask, dwFlags
		CreateIoCompletionPort gives 0  // 4: FileHandle, ExistingCompletionPort, CompletionKey, NumberOfConcurrentThreads
		SetFileCompletionNotificationModes gives 0  // 2: FileHandle, Flags
		CreateEventA gives 0  // 4: lpEventAttributes, bManualReset, bInitialState, lpName
		SetEvent gives 0  // 1: hEvent
		InitializeCriticalSection gives 0  // 1: lpCriticalSection
		EnterCriticalSection gives 0  // 1: lpCriticalSection
		LeaveCriticalSection gives 0  // 1: lpCriticalSection
		GetLastError gives 0  // 0
		GetCurrentProcessId gives 0  // 0
		CancelIoEx gives 0  // 2: hFile, lpOverlapped
		GetSystemTimeAsFileTime gives 0  // 1: lpSystemTimeAsFileTime
		GetFileSizeEx gives 0  // 2: hFile, lpFileSize
		DeleteFileA gives 0  // 1: lpFileName
		MoveFileExA gives 0  // 3: lpExistingFileName, lpNewFileName, dwFlags
		FindFirstFileA gives 0  // 2: lpFileName, lpFindFileData
		FindClose gives 0  // 1: hFindFile
		FindNextFileA gives 0  // 2: hFindFile, lpFindFileData
		CreateDirectoryA gives 0  // 2: lpPathName, lpSecurityAttributes
		GetCurrentDirectoryA gives 0  // 2: nBufferLength, lpBuffer
		GetFileAttributesA gives 0  // 1: lpFileName
		GetCommandLineA gives 0  // 0
		GetModuleFileNameA gives 0  // 3: hModule, lpFilename, nSize
		GetConsoleMode gives 0  // 2: hConsoleHandle, lpMode

		// The console writer's two >4-argument imports. Both counts are declared beside their siblings in
		// `StdToX64Conversion` and read from there, so the shim's copy count and `mrt_write_stream`'s own
		// outgoing reservation are one fact.
		WriteConsoleW gives WriteConsoleStackArgWords  // 5: hConsoleOutput, lpBuffer, nNumberOfCharsToWrite, lpNumberOfCharsWritten, lpReserved
		MultiByteToWideChar gives MultiByteToWideCharStackArgWords  // 6: CodePage, dwFlags, lpMultiByteStr, cbMultiByte, lpWideCharStr, cchWideChar

		// O3. Its fourth sibling `MapViewOfFile` takes five and is stated with the other non-zero counts above.
		GetEnvironmentVariableA gives 0  // 3: lpName, lpBuffer, nSize
		OpenFileMappingA gives 0  // 3: dwDesiredAccess, bInheritHandle, lpName
		GetTickCount64 gives 0  // 0

		// The two attached-subprocess imports. `PeekNamedPipe` takes SIX arguments — four in registers and
		// two on the stack — so it is the only one of this pair with a non-zero count.
		PeekNamedPipe gives PeekPipeStackArgWords  // 6: hNamedPipe, lpBuffer, nBufferSize, lpBytesRead, lpTotalBytesAvail, lpBytesLeftThisMessage
		TerminateProcess gives 0  // 2: hProcess, uExitCode

		// ⭐ **THE EIGHT `ws2_32` SOCKET IMPORTS — EVERY ONE TAKES FOUR ARGUMENTS OR FEWER**, so every one
		// passes entirely in registers and needs no stack words. That is not a coincidence worth relying on
		// silently: `getaddrinfo`, `send` and `recv` sit exactly AT the four-register limit, so a future
		// socket import with a fifth argument (`WSARecv`, `select`) must state its count here like
		// `PeekNamedPipe` does, or the shim will hand it a frame too small and it will read the caller's.
		WSAStartup gives 0  // 2: wVersionRequested, lpWSAData
		getaddrinfo gives 0  // 4: pNodeName, pServiceName, pHints, ppResult
		freeaddrinfo gives 0  // 1: pAddrInfo
		socket gives 0  // 3: af, type, protocol
		connect gives 0  // 3: s, name, namelen
		send gives 0  // 4: s, buf, len, flags
		recv gives 0  // 4: s, buf, len, flags
		closesocket gives 0  // 1: s
		GetActiveProcessorCount gives 0  // 1: GroupNumber

		// ⚠ **THE SHIM SWITCHES STACKS, NOT THREADS**, which is the one thing that could have made this
		// import wrong here: `QueryThreadCycleTime` reports the CALLING THREAD's CPU time, and a green thread
		// runs on the same M whichever stack its frame sits on. Its out-param is a `.data` word rather than a
		// stack slot besides, so the switch cannot move the buffer under it either.
		QueryThreadCycleTime gives 0  // 2: ThreadHandle, CycleTime

		// G1: both TLS imports are register-only. ⚠ **NEITHER IS EVER SHIMMED IN PRACTICE** — `TlsAlloc` runs
		// once in `__gt_init` and `TlsSetValue` at each M's adoption, and every one of those sites executes on
		// an OS thread whose current GT has `stackBase == 0`, which is exactly the shim's direct-call guard. The
		// count is stated anyway because this match is TOTAL: an import with no arm is a compile error, which is
		// what keeps a later >4-argument import from being handed a frame too small.
		TlsAlloc gives 0  // 0
		TlsSetValue gives 0  // 2: dwTlsIndex, lpTlsValue

		// The priority pair. Both are register-only, and unlike the TLS pair these CAN be shimmed in practice:
		// a harness may ask to be de-prioritised from inside a green thread, whose stack is the small one.
		SetPriorityClass gives 0  // 2: hProcess, dwPriorityClass
		GetPriorityClass gives 0  // 1: hProcess

		// The whole-environment pair, both register-only. A spawn that names an override reaches them from
		// inside a green thread — the harness's own run step is one — so, like the priority pair, they really
		// are shimmed onto the system stack in practice.
		GetEnvironmentStringsA gives 0  // 0
		FreeEnvironmentStringsA gives 0  // 1: lpszEnvironmentBlock

		// The terminal question, register-only. A harness asks it from its own main green thread before it
		// prints anything, so it really is shimmed onto the system stack in practice.
		GetFileType gives 0  // 1: hFile

		// The named-section pair. `CreateFileMappingA` takes SIX arguments — four in registers and two on the
		// stack — so it is the only one of this pair with a non-zero count, derived from the same constant
		// `stdOpOutgoingArgBytes` reserves the frame with.
		CreateFileMappingA gives CreateFileMappingStackArgWords  // 6: hFile, lpFileMappingAttributes, flProtect, dwMaximumSizeHigh, dwMaximumSizeLow, lpName
		UnmapViewOfFile gives 0  // 1: lpBaseAddress

		// The process-sampling band. THREE of the thirteen overflow the four argument registers: the two
		// six-argument services answer the same constant `stdOpOutgoingArgBytes` reserves the frame with —
		// `osHostSample` is ONE op whose reservation must hold its WIDEST member — and the five-argument one
		// states its own, because this table keys on a SLOT and copies exactly what that call reads.
		NtGetNextThread gives HostSampleStackArgWords  // 6: ProcessHandle, ThreadHandle, DesiredAccess, HandleAttributes, Flags, NewThreadHandle
		SetWaitableTimer gives HostSampleStackArgWords  // 6: hTimer, lpDueTime, lPeriod, pfnCompletionRoutine, lpArgToCompletionRoutine, fResume
		ReadProcessMemory gives ReadProcessMemoryStackArgWords  // 5: hProcess, lpBaseAddress, lpBuffer, nSize, lpNumberOfBytesRead
		OpenProcess gives 0  // 3: dwDesiredAccess, bInheritHandle, dwProcessId
		SuspendThread gives 0  // 1: hThread
		ResumeThread gives 0  // 1: hThread
		GetThreadContext gives 0  // 2: hThread, lpContext
		SetThreadContext gives 0  // 2: hThread, lpContext
		OpenThread gives 0  // 3: dwDesiredAccess, bInheritHandle, dwThreadId
		GetCurrentThreadId gives 0  // 0
		GetThreadId gives 0  // 1: Thread
		VirtualQueryEx gives 0  // 4: hProcess, lpAddress, lpBuffer, dwLength
		CreateWaitableTimerExW gives 0  // 4: lpTimerAttributes, lpTimerName, dwFlags, dwDesiredAccess
		CreateToolhelp32Snapshot gives 0  // 2: dwFlags, th32ProcessID
		Module32FirstW gives 0  // 2: hSnapshot, lpme
		Module32NextW gives 0  // 2: hSnapshot, lpme

		// The poller's pair. The wait takes six, so two on the stack; the break four.
		GetQueuedCompletionStatusEx gives GetQueuedCompletionStatusExStackArgWords  // 6: CompletionPort, lpCompletionPortEntries, ulCount, ulNumEntriesRemoved, dwMilliseconds, fAlertable
		PostQueuedCompletionStatus gives 0  // 4: CompletionPort, dwNumberOfBytesTransferred, dwCompletionKey, lpOverlapped
		SwitchToThread gives 0  // 0
	end 'k'
end 'gtShimStackArgWordsOf'

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE SYSCALL BRACKET (W213-C3) — WHICH IMPORTS MAY COST THIS MACHINE ITS PROCESSOR
// ==========================================================================

// ⛔⛔ **THE CLASSIFICATION IS MANDATORY, AND HERE IS THE CASE THAT PROVES IT RATHER THAN THE PRINCIPLE THAT
// SUGGESTS IT.** `EnterCriticalSection` **is** reached through this shim — from `__mbox_send`, `__mbox_recv`,
// `__gt_timer_add`, and `__slab_lock` on EVERY allocation a green thread makes. Bracket it and:
//
//   1. a green thread on M1/P1 blocks acquiring `__sched_lock`, because another machine holds it;
//   2. `__sysmon` sees the same generation past the threshold and `__sched_handoffp` takes P1 away;
//   3. M1 finally acquires the lock, leaves the call and finds `MSyscallStolen`;
//   4. `__sched_syscall_regain` waits for a processor — **while M1 holds `__sched_lock`**;
//   5. the lock is now held for ever by a machine that cannot run. **Whole-scheduler deadlock.**
//
// ⇒ **the rule this table encodes is *"`blocking` means it can wait an unbounded time on something OUTSIDE
// the scheduler"*, and its contrapositive is a REQUIREMENT ON THE RUNTIME: a `blocking` import may never be
// called with `__sched_lock` held.** Every scheduler-internal call under that lock — the timer walk's
// `WaitForMultipleObjects`, the process walk's `GetExitCodeProcess`, the register's `CloseHandle`, the
// completion loop's own imports — is `schedulerBound` below, which is what makes the requirement checkable by
// reading one table rather than by tracing every lock bracket.
//
// ⚠ **THE `match` IS TOTAL OVER `IatSlot` AND HAS NO `default` ARM, WHICH IS `gtShimStackArgWordsOf`'s OWN
// STATED RULE ONE FUNCTION UP AND FOR THE SAME MEASURED REASON.** That table's silent `return 0` once handed
// `CreateFileA` a frame too small and let the kernel read whatever was on the stack; a `default gives
// schedulerBound` here would be the same defect wearing a `match`'s clothes — a new blocking import would
// silently keep costing its processor, and nothing would say so.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE `SyscallClass` VOCABULARY IS `StdDialect`'s, AND `StdDialect.stdOpSyscallClassOf` IS THE SOURCE
// OF TRUTH FOR THE RULE THIS TABLE APPLIES.** The two tables are not one fact written twice: that one keys on
// a Std OP, this one on an IAT SLOT — a Win32 entry point — because this lane's bracket sits BELOW the StdOp
// level, inline in the import shim, where the only thing left to classify is the slot. Moving this lane's
// bracket up to the StdOp door would delete this table; it needs a Windows host to re-mint its goldens, so it
// is not this change.
//
// The syscall class of ONE import, exhaustive over `IatSlot`. See the header above.
function gtShimSyscallClassOf(slot IatSlot) returns SyscallClass
	return match slot 'k'
		// --- `blocking`: the calls a green thread can sit inside for an unbounded time ---
		//
		// ⭐ **`WriteFile` IS THE HOTTEST BRACKETED SLOT THERE IS — every `print` from a green thread — which is
		// why the bracket has to stay at one exchange each way and must not touch `__sched_lock`.** It is here
		// and not with the bounded family because a write to a pipe whose reader has stopped reading blocks
		// exactly as long as the reader takes, which is a property of the far end and not of this process.
		WriteFile gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// ⛔ **A CONSOLE IN QUICK-EDIT SELECTION SUSPENDS ITS WRITER**, unbounded by the far end exactly as a
		// stalled pipe reader is — so this is `WriteFile`'s position and not `GetConsoleMode`'s.
		WriteConsoleW gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// ⭐⭐ **`ReadFile` IS THIS RUNG'S SUBJECT.** `__con_read_stdin` is a plain synchronous read of standard
		// input with no overlapped IO and no park (`ConsoleRuntime`), so a green thread inside it holds its OS
		// thread in the kernel until the user presses return — which is what
		// `specs/sched-syscall-handoff.md`'s ordering case measures.
		ReadFile gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// The file-system family. Every one of them can reach a cold disk, a network share or a stalled device.
		CreateFileA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		CreateFileW gives SyscallClass.blocking
		CreateNamedPipeW gives SyscallClass.blocking
		GetFileSizeEx gives SyscallClass.blocking
		DeleteFileA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		MoveFileExA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		FindFirstFileA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		FindNextFileA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		CreateDirectoryA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		GetCurrentDirectoryA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		GetFileAttributesA gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// The file-MAPPING pair, which fault pages in from the same devices.
		OpenFileMappingA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		MapViewOfFile gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// Spawning a child image and peeking a child's pipe both wait on another process.
		CreateProcessA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		PeekNamedPipe gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// The socket family. `getaddrinfo` waits on DNS, `connect`/`send`/`recv` on a peer, `closesocket` on
		// whatever `SO_LINGER` was set to, and `WSAStartup` loads and initialises the provider stack.
		WSAStartup gives SyscallClass.blocking
		getaddrinfo gives SyscallClass.blocking
		socket gives SyscallClass.blocking
		connect gives SyscallClass.blocking
		send gives SyscallClass.blocking
		recv gives SyscallClass.blocking
		closesocket gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// --- `schedulerBound`: the scheduler's own machinery, and the bounded-and-short remainder ---
		//
		// ⛔ The lock pair is the deadlock this table's header sets out in five steps. Do not move them.
		EnterCriticalSection gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		LeaveCriticalSection gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		InitializeCriticalSection gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// ⛔⛔ **THE WAITS ARE THE SCHEDULER WAITING FOR ITS OWN WORK.** An idle machine's park and
		// `__sched_syscall_regain`'s wait go through them holding no processor, so there is none for `__sysmon`
		// to take.
		WaitForSingleObject gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		WaitForMultipleObjects gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// ⚠ **THIS ARM IS INERT AND SAYS SO RATHER THAN BEING LEFT TO LOOK LIVE**, exactly as the same slot's
		// stack-arg arm does one function up: the completion loop is hand-assembled and calls
		// `GetQueuedCompletionStatus` DIRECTLY (`StdToX64Conversion`), so it never reaches this shim. It is
		// classified anyway because this `match` is TOTAL — and `schedulerBound` is the right answer if it ever
		// did, since that loop is the scheduler waiting on its own completions.
		GetQueuedCompletionStatus gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// Process teardown, memory, events, handles and the clocks: bounded and short, every one.
		ExitProcess gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetStdHandle gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// ⚠ A "file" call that is NOT in the blocking family above, and the distinction is the ARGUMENT: the
		// path-taking `GetFileAttributesA` can reach a cold disk, while this one answers the type of an
		// ALREADY-OPEN handle out of the file object the kernel is holding — bounded and short, like the
		// `GetStdHandle` that produced the handle it is asked about.
		GetFileType gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		AddVectoredExceptionHandler gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		VirtualAlloc gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		VirtualFree gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		QueryPerformanceCounter gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		QueryPerformanceFrequency gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetSystemTimeAsFileTime gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetTickCount64 gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		QueryThreadCycleTime gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetExitCodeProcess gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		TerminateProcess gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		CloseHandle gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SetHandleInformation gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		CreateIoCompletionPort gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SetFileCompletionNotificationModes gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		CancelIoEx gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		FindClose gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		CreateEventA gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SetEvent gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// ⚠ **`CreateThread` IS THE ONE THAT LOOKS LIKE IT BELONGS ABOVE AND DOES NOT.** Its only caller is
		// `__sched_startm`'s spawn arm, which runs INSIDE `__sched_lock` — so bracketing it would be the
		// header's five-step deadlock reached by a second road. It is bounded in any case: creating a thread
		// commits a stack and returns, and nothing about it waits on another party.
		CreateThread gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetLastError gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetCurrentProcessId gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetCommandLineA gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetModuleFileNameA gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// ⚠ **`GetConsoleMode` IS `GetFileType`'s POSITION EXACTLY** — it answers out of the file object the
		// kernel already holds for a handle this process owns, so it is bounded and short.
		GetConsoleMode gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// ⚠ **NOT A SYSTEM CALL AT ALL** — a table walk in user-mode kernel32 over the caller's own bytes.
		MultiByteToWideChar gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetEnvironmentVariableA gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetEnvironmentStringsA gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		FreeEnvironmentStringsA gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetActiveProcessorCount gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SetPriorityClass gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetPriorityClass gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		TlsAlloc gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		TlsSetValue gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		freeaddrinfo gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// The named-section pair. Creating a section reaches the memory manager and can touch the page file,
		// exactly as `OpenFileMappingA`/`MapViewOfFile` above; tearing a view down waits on nothing.
		CreateFileMappingA gives SyscallClass.blocking
		UnmapViewOfFile gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// The process-sampling band splits on ONE question: does the call reach memory the kernel may have
		// to fault in, or another process's loader data? A stack window copied out of a suspended thread can
		// touch a paged-out page, and a module walk reads the target's loader list under its own lock — both
		// are `MapViewOfFile`'s position, one question over.
		ReadProcessMemory gives SyscallClass.blocking
		CreateToolhelp32Snapshot gives SyscallClass.blocking
		Module32FirstW gives SyscallClass.blocking
		Module32NextW gives SyscallClass.blocking

		// The remainder is bounded kernel bookkeeping over handles this process already holds: a handle
		// open, the suspend/resume pair and the context read, the thread walk, the VAD query and the timer
		// pair. None of them waits on another party — the sampler's WAIT is `osWaitHandle`'s
		// `WaitForSingleObject`, classified above.
		OpenProcess gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SuspendThread gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		ResumeThread gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetThreadContext gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SetThreadContext gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		OpenThread gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetCurrentThreadId gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		GetThreadId gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		VirtualQueryEx gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		NtGetNextThread gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		CreateWaitableTimerExW gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		SetWaitableTimer gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// The scheduler's poller — `StdDialect.stdOpSyscallClassOf`'s `osNetpoll` arm carries why its wait is
		// the scheduler waiting for its own work.
		GetQueuedCompletionStatusEx gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
		PostQueuedCompletionStatus gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound

		// A yield gives the rest of this quantum away and comes straight back; it waits on no other party.
		SwitchToThread gives SyscallClass.schedulerBound
	end 'k'
end 'gtShimSyscallClassOf'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`entersyscall`, INLINE — *"this machine is about to be unreachable; its processor may be taken."***
// Emitted immediately before `encodeIatCall`, after the TIB has been repointed at the system stack, which is
// the last point at which anything is free to clobber.
//
// ⛔ **IT CANNOT BE A CALL, AND THAT IS NOT A PERFORMANCE ARGUMENT.** In the pre-call window
// rcx/rdx/r8/r9 hold live Win64 arguments and the stack arguments are already laid out at their exact
// displacements; a `call` would need shadow space carved out of a frame whose shape the kernel is about to
// read. Inline it is TWO instructions plus the three-instruction TLS walk, all of it in rax and r11 — the two
// registers the shim already treats as dead here (rax has just been destroyed by the TIB repoint, and r11's
// last use was the stack-arg copy shuttle).
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE `lock add` DOES BOTH HALVES, BECAUSE THE GENERATION AND THE STATE SHARE A WORD.**
// `MSyscallEnterDelta` steps the generation AND sets the in-call bit in a single indivisible step, so there is
// no interval in which a new call is announced without its generation or the other way round — which is what
// an earlier two-word cut of this got wrong (`SchedRuntime.MOffSyscallState` carries the ABA that cost).
//
// ⛔ **THE `lock` PREFIX IS REQUIRED AND IS NOT AN ABUNDANCE OF CAUTION.** This word's other writer is
// `__sched_retake`'s compare-and-swap; an unlocked read-modify-write here could lose the thief's mark and
// leave this machine believing it still owns a processor that has been handed to somebody else.
//
// ⚠ **THE M IS RELOADED FROM TLS RATHER THAN CARRIED**, because the guard's copy lived in rax and the TIB
// repoint destroyed it. It cannot be null and it cannot bail: `emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless` carries the proof,
// which is the shim's own guard three loads above.
function emitGtEnterSyscall(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray)
	emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.rax, os: Os.windows)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: ShimGtReg, value: MSyscallEnterDelta as MachineWord)
	encodeLockedRmwBaseDispReg(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, base: X64Register.rax, disp: MOffSyscallState as Imm32, reg: ShimGtReg)
end 'emitGtEnterSyscall'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`exitsyscall`, INLINE, WITH A CALL OUT ONLY WHEN THE PROCESSOR IS GONE.** Emitted at the very END of
// the shim — after the TIB restore, after `add rsp, reserve`, and after RSP has been switched back to the GT
// stack and the caller's r10 popped — which is where it must be for two independent reasons:
//
//   • **A CALL FROM THE SYSTEM STACK WOULD DESTROY ITS OWN RETURN ADDRESS.** At that point RSP is the system
//     stack's TOP, and any shimmed import the callee makes re-enters this shim, which sets RSP to that same
//     top and overwrites the word the `call` had just pushed. Nesting the shim is not safe and this is not the
//     rung that makes it safe; running the slow path from the GT stack sidesteps it entirely.
//   • **THE FAST PATH COSTS THE SAME EITHER WAY.** It is one TLS walk, one `lock xadd`, one `and` and one
//     branch, and the registers it needs (rcx and r11) are dead on both roads.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`lock xadd` ANSWERS BOTH QUESTIONS AT ONCE, WHICH IS WHY THERE IS NO RETRY LOOP AND NO `cmpxchg`.**
// Subtracting `MSyscallInCall` clears the in-call bit, and the instruction hands back the PRIOR word — whose
// stolen bit is the entire verdict. A `cmpxchg` retry would have been correct too, and was rejected for two
// reasons: it claims `rax`, which holds the kernel's RESULT, and it needs a backward branch inside a chunk
// whose label table is forward-only.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE STOLEN BIT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* CLEARED HERE — `__sched_syscall_regain` clears it.** Leaving it
// set is what marks this machine as *"holding a green thread with no processor"* for the two parties that must
// see that state: `__sched_retake`, which requires exactly `MSyscallInCall` and therefore skips it, and
// `__sched_stop_workers`, which must NOT join a machine that may never come back.
// `SchedRuntime.MOffSyscallState` carries both halves.
//
// ⚠ **`rax` IS THE KERNEL'S RESULT AND SURVIVES BOTH ROADS.** The exchange deliberately uses rcx; the slow
// path parks the result in its own frame across the call.
//
// ⚠ **THE 0x30 FRAME IS 0x20 OF WIN64 SHADOW SPACE PLUS ONE SAVED WORD, ROUNDED TO KEEP RSP 16-ALIGNED AT THE
// `call`** — the compiler's frame invariant at every call site, which the shim's own entry comment states and which
// the `sub` here must not break.
function emitGtExitSyscall(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray)
	emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: ShimGtReg, os: Os.windows)

	// `mov reg, imm64` and not the 32-bit form: `-MSyscallInCall` is NEGATIVE, and `mov ecx, imm32`
	// ZERO-extends — it would add 0x00000000FFFFFFFF to the word rather than subtracting one from it.
	encodeMovRegImm64(code, dest: ShimTibScratchReg, value: (0 - MSyscallInCall) as MachineWord)
	encodeLockedRmwBaseDispReg(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeXaddRmReg, base: ShimGtReg, disp: MOffSyscallState as Imm32, reg: ShimTibScratchReg)

	// rcx now holds the PRIOR word. Its stolen bit is the verdict; everything above it is the generation and
	// is none of this side's business, which is what the mask is for.
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAnd, reg: ShimTibScratchReg, imm: MSyscallStolen as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: ShimDoneLabel)

	encodePrologue(code, stackSize: exitSyscallSlowFrameBytes(), probeScratchIsFree: false)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: ExitSyscallSavedResultDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: SchedSyscallRegainName)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: ExitSyscallSavedResultDisp, wide: true)
	encodeEpilogue(code, stackSize: exitSyscallSlowFrameBytes())
end 'emitGtExitSyscall'

// The slow path's own frame: Win64 shadow space plus one word for the kernel's result, rounded up to the
// stack alignment so RSP is 16-aligned at the `call`. Derived from the three constants that decide it rather
// than written as `0x30`, for the reason the shim's `frameSize` is derived from its two — and a FUNCTION
// rather than a `let` because a global initializer may not call one (E2045).
function exitSyscallSlowFrameBytes() returns StackSize
	return alignUpStack(ShadowSpaceBytes + SpillSlotBytes, alignment: StackFrameAlignment)
end 'exitSyscallSlowFrameBytes'

// Where in that frame the result waits — immediately above the shadow space, which is the first byte the
// callee may not touch.
let ExitSyscallSavedResultDisp = ShadowSpaceBytes as Imm32

// Branch to `bail` unless `addr` lies inside `gt`'s own stack, `[stackBase, stackBase + stackSize)`; a `stackBase`
// of 0 bails too. Past both bails `scratch` holds the stack's top and `gt` its size.
module function emitGtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack(code ByteArray, fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, gt X64Register, scratch X64Register, addr X64Register, bail BlockId)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: scratch, base: gt, disp: GtOffStackBase as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: scratch, src: scratch, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: bail)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: addr, src: scratch, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.below, targetBlockId: bail)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: gt, base: gt, disp: GtOffStackSize as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: scratch, src: gt, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: addr, src: scratch, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetBlockId: bail)
end 'emitGtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack'

// Emit an `iatCall`, wrapped in the system-stack shim iff the module carries the GT scheduler (see the header).
export function emitGtGatedIatCall(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, slotIndex RelocSlotIndex, usesGt bool)
	if usesGt 'shim'
		emitGtShimmedIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, callFixups: callFixups, slotIndex: slotIndex)
	end 'shim' else 'plain'
		encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: slotIndex)
	end 'plain'
end 'emitGtGatedIatCall'

// The shim itself. RSP is 16-aligned at entry (the compiler's frame invariant at every call), and the switch makes RSP
// 16-aligned at the `call` INDEPENDENTLY of the caller's alignment (it sets RSP = the page-aligned system-stack
// top and reserves a frame chosen to land on 16). Disassembly of the CreateProcessA (6 stack args) path:
//   lea rax,[rip+__sched_tls_teb_offset] ; mov rax,[rax] ; test rax,rax ; jz direct  (no scheduler → direct)
//   mov rax,gs:[rax]              ; test rax,rax ; jz direct                   (this OS thread is no M)
//   mov r11,[rax+0x08]            ; test r11,r11 ; jz direct                   (no current GT)
//   mov rax,[r11+0x18]            ; test rax,rax ; jz direct                   (stackBase==0 → real OS stack)
//   cmp rsp,rax ; jb direct ; mov r11,[r11+0x20] ; add rax,r11 ; cmp rsp,rax ; jae direct  (RSP off the GT stack)
//   lea rax,[rip+__sched_tls_teb_offset] ; mov rax,[rax] ; mov rax,gs:[rax]    (the M again)
//   push r10 ; mov r10,rsp ; mov rax,[rax+0x10] ; mov rsp,rax                  (this M's OWN syscall stack)
//   push r10 ; sub rsp,0x58                                                    (shadow 0x20 + 6 args 0x30 + pad 8)
//   (mov r11,[r10+0x28+k*8] ; mov [rsp+0x20+k*8],r11) for k in 0..6            (6 args → [rsp+0x20..0x48])
//   mov gs:[0x08],rax ; sub rax,0x10000 ; mov gs:[0x10],rax                    (repoint TIB at the system stack)
//   call [CreateProcessA]
//   lea r11,[rip+__sched_tls_teb_offset] ; mov r11,[r11] ; mov r11,gs:[r11] ; mov r11,[r11+0x08]
//   mov rcx,[r11+0x90] ; mov gs:[0x08],rcx ; mov rcx,[r11+0x98] ; mov gs:[0x10],rcx   (restore TIB to this GT)
//   add rsp,0x58 ; pop r10 ; mov rsp,r10 ; pop r10 ; jmp done                  (switch back to the GT stack)
//   direct: call [CreateProcessA]
//   done:
// `[m + MOffLocks] += delta` — the shim's bracket over the window where `m` names a machine this thread may
// stop belonging to. See the call sites for why it is unlocked.
//
// ⭐ **A PLAIN READ-MODIFY-WRITE, NOT A LOCKED ONE, AND THAT IS ARGUED RATHER THAN SAVED.** This word is
// written only by the thread that IS this machine, and the only other party READS it while that thread is
// SUSPENDED (or, on a POSIX lane, from a handler running on the thread itself) — so there is no interleaving
// for a lock prefix to prevent, and these are hand-assembled bytes no pass can reorder.
function emitShimLockDepthStep(code ByteArray, m X64Register, delta ParsedInt)
	encodeGrp1BaseDispImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, base: m, disp: MOffLocks as Imm32, imm: delta as Imm32)
end 'emitShimLockDepthStep'

// `__gt_shim_enter() -> rax = m`, with `m.locks` ALREADY RAISED — see the shim's call site for the boundary
// this exists to delete. Its caller has already proven there is a machine (`emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail`
// answered non-null a moment ago and a thread never stops being one), so the walk is the bail-less one.
//
// ⚠ **IT CLOBBERS RAX AND THE FLAGS AND NOTHING ELSE.** At the call site rcx/rdx/r8/r9 carry the kernel
// call's own arguments, r10 the caller's value and r11 the current green thread; all four survive. The
// flags are dead there — the guard above ends in a conditional branch.
//
// ⚠ **ITS RETURN ADDRESS IS THE ONLY THING IT PUTS ON THE GREEN THREAD'S STACK, AND IT IS GONE BEFORE THE
// SHIM PUSHES ANYTHING** — so the peak `x64ShimmedImportStep` reports is unchanged.
function buildGtShimEnterChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var rdataRelocs = RdataRelocationArray.create()

	emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.rax, os: target.os)
	emitShimLockDepthStep(code, m: X64Register.rax, delta: OneMore)
	code.push(OpcodeRet)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(GtShimEnterName, code: code, callFixups: CallFixupArray.create(), rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(), rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtShimEnterChunk'

function emitGtShimmedIatCall(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, slotIndex RelocSlotIndex)
	// ⭐ **THE SLOT IS RESOLVED ONCE AND ASKED TWO QUESTIONS** — how many stack argument words it takes, and
	// whether it may cost this machine its processor. Both are COMPILE-TIME constants here, so the syscall
	// bracket below is emitted or not emitted per import with no runtime dispatch whatever.
	let slot = iatSlotOfIndex(slotIndex)
	let stackArgWords = gtShimStackArgWordsOf(slot)
	let bracketed = gtShimSyscallClassOf(slot) == SyscallClass.blocking

	// reserve = (16-aligned shadow + stack args) + one pushed word — lands RSP 16-aligned at the `call` after
	// the two pushes.
	// ⚠ THE STRIDE IS `SpillSlotBytes` — the SAME constant `win64OutgoingArgDisp` places the ISEL's arg stores
	// with (StdToX64Conversion). The copy below must read and write the exact addresses that rule computed, so
	// the two ends are ONE number — not another constant that happens to be 8, which would leave the shim
	// copying from and to slots the isel never wrote the day the outgoing-arg grid widened.
	// ⚠ **DELIBERATELY NOT `win64OutgoingArgDisp(stackArgWords)`, THOUGH THE ARITHMETIC IS IDENTICAL.** This is
	// a frame SIZE — how many bytes the shim must reserve — and that function answers a DISPLACEMENT, where one
	// named slot begins. They coincide because the frame ends exactly where the next slot would start, and
	// folding them would make a size and an address one expression on the strength of a coincidence. Both
	// derive from the same two constants, which is the part that has to be true.
	let frameSize = alignUpStack(ShadowSpaceBytes + (stackArgWords * SpillSlotBytes), alignment: StackFrameAlignment)
	let reserve = frameSize + ShimPushedWordBytes

	var shimFixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var shimLabels = BlockOffsetTable.create()

	// Guard: no M (no scheduler yet, or an OS thread that is not one), no current GT, or RSP not on the current
	// GT's own stack → run the call directly. The last covers a context already on a real OS-thread stack
	// (`stackBase == 0` — an M's inline scheduler GT) and a fault handler, which runs wherever the fault
	// happened — the system stack included, where a switch to its TOP would overwrite the frames the handler
	// returns into. See `Arm64GtRuntime.buildArm64DarwinLibcCallChunk` for the same test on the other lane.
	//
	// rax is caller-saved and the coming call clobbers it anyway; it carries the M out of the guard, where the
	// system-stack top is read out of it after the two pushes below.
	emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, fixups: shimFixups, mDest: X64Register.rax, gtDest: ShimGtReg, bail: ShimDirectLabel, os: Os.windows)
	emitGtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack(code, fixups: shimFixups, gt: ShimGtReg, scratch: X64Register.rax, addr: X64Register.rsp, bail: ShimDirectLabel)

	// ⛔⛔⛔ **THE MACHINE IS IN A REGISTER FROM HERE UNTIL RSP LEAVES THE GREEN THREAD'S STACK, AND THAT
	// WINDOW MUST NOT BE STOPPED IN.** An asynchronous preemption parks the thread wherever it stands and it
	// may resume on a DIFFERENT machine — where `rax` would name the machine it left, and the switch below
	// would put this thread's kernel call on ANOTHER machine's 64 KB system stack while that machine is using
	// it. `MOffLocks` is exactly the word the safe-point predicate refuses on, so the window is bracketed
	// with it.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE READ AND THE RAISE ARE ONE CALL, BECAUSE THE BOUNDARY BETWEEN THEM IS ITSELF A SAFE
	// POINT.** Written inline — `mov rax, gs:[…]` then `add [rax + locks], 1` — there is an instruction
	// boundary at which the machine is cached and the depth is NOT yet raised: a stop there is a safe point
	// by every clause of the predicate, and the thread that resumes elsewhere then steps ANOTHER machine's
	// counter (a lost update leaves that machine permanently unpreemptible) before switching onto its stack.
	// No ordering of a "read m, then step m's counter" pair closes it; only moving both inside code that is
	// never a safe point does. `__gt_shim_enter` is a hand-assembled chunk, and a chunk's `__symtable`
	// safe-point byte is 0 — so a stop lands either BEFORE the call, with nothing cached, or AFTER it, with
	// the depth already raised.
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtShimEnterName)

	// Switch RSP to THIS M's OWN system stack (`SchedRuntime.MOffSystemStackSP` carries why it is the M's). r10
	// = the GT rsp anchor (points at the pushed caller-r10 word); rax holds the system-stack top through the
	// copy and the TIB repoint below.
	encodePushReg(code, reg: ShimGtRspReg)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: ShimGtRspReg, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rsp, base: X64Register.rax, disp: MOffSystemStackSP as Imm32, wide: true)

	// The release, and it needs no chunk of its own: RSP is already off the green thread's stack, so the
	// safe-point predicate's range test refuses a stop here whatever the depth says.
	emitShimLockDepthStep(code, m: X64Register.rax, delta: OneFewer)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	encodePushReg(code, reg: ShimGtRspReg)

	// sub rsp, reserve — the outgoing-arg frame on the system stack. Shadow space plus one import's
	// stack-argument words (`gtShimStackArgWordsOf`, SIX at the widest — `CreateProcessStackArgWords`), so it
	// is well inside one page; r11 is LIVE across it (currentGt, reloaded from below after the call), hence no
	// certificate — a shim whose reservation ever reached a page would panic rather than eat it.
	encodePrologue(code, stackSize: reserve, probeScratchIsFree: false)

	// Copy the N true stack args from the GT frame (`[r10 + 8 + 0x20 + k*8]`, the anchor being 8 below the GT
	// rsp) to the system stack (`[rsp + 0x20 + k*8]`), so the kernel reads its args from where it runs.
	for k in 0 upto stackArgWords 'copyArg'
		// Both ends ask `win64OutgoingArgDisp` — the SAME rule the ISEL placed these words by — so the copy
		// cannot address a different slot than the store did. The source adds only `ShimPushedWordBytes`,
		// because the GT-frame anchor sits one pushed word below the GT rsp; that is a property of THIS shim's
		// prologue and not of the arg layout, which is why it is the one term added here rather than folded in.
		let dstDisp = win64OutgoingArgDisp(k)
		let srcDisp = (ShimPushedWordBytes + (dstDisp as StackSize)) as Imm32
		encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: ShimGtReg, base: ShimGtRspReg, disp: srcDisp, wide: true)
		encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: dstDisp, srcReg: ShimGtReg)
	end 'copyArg'

	// Repoint the TIB at the system stack for the call (rax = system-stack top, destroyed here — it is dead
	// before the call, which clobbers the caller-saved rax anyway).
	emitRepointTibToSystemStack(code, topReg: X64Register.rax)

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SYSCALL BRACKET'S NEAR SIDE (W213-C3), FOR A `blocking` IMPORT ONLY.** It is emitted HERE —
	// after the TIB repoint and immediately before the call — because rax and r11 are dead at exactly this
	// point and nowhere earlier: rax carried the M through the guard and the system-stack top through the
	// switch, and r11 was the stack-arg copy shuttle. See `emitGtEnterSyscall`.
	if bracketed 'armTheHandoff'
		emitGtEnterSyscall(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs)
	end 'armTheHandoff'

	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: slotIndex)

	// Restore the TIB to this GT's own bounds. Reload the M (the call clobbered both r11 and rax), read
	// `currentGt` back out of it, and use rcx as scratch so rax — the kernel call's RESULT, read by the op
	// after the shim — survives. The restore IS a TIB-from-GT load, the same operation the context switch does
	// on switch-in. It cannot bail; see `emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS IS THE READ W213 WOULD HAVE BROKEN (C1).** It happens AFTER a kernel call that may have
	// blocked for an arbitrarily long time — precisely the window a handoff takes a P in — so answering it
	// through the P would have handed this M whatever green thread the P's NEW M had since published. Through
	// the M it is this thread's own answer by construction.
	emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: ShimGtReg, os: Os.windows)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: ShimGtReg, base: ShimGtReg, disp: MOffCurrentGt as Imm32, wide: true)
	emitLoadTibFromGt(code, gtReg: ShimGtReg, scratch: ShimTibScratchReg)

	// Switch RSP back to the GT stack, restore the caller's r10, and skip the direct-call path.
	encodeEpilogue(code, stackSize: reserve)  // add rsp, reserve
	encodePopReg(code, reg: ShimGtRspReg)  // r10 = the saved GT rsp (points at the pushed caller-r10)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsp, src: ShimGtRspReg, wide: true)
	encodePopReg(code, reg: ShimGtRspReg)  // restore the caller's r10

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SYSCALL BRACKET'S FAR SIDE (W213-C3), ON THE GT STACK.** It is the LAST thing the shimmed road
	// does — `emitGtExitSyscall` carries why it may not run one instruction earlier, on the system stack — and
	// its own not-stolen arm branches straight to `done`, so the `jmp` below serves only the slow path.
	if bracketed 'collectTheHandoff'
		emitGtExitSyscall(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, callFixups: callFixups, fixups: shimFixups)
	end 'collectTheHandoff'

	encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: shimFixups, targetBlockId: ShimDoneLabel)

	// direct: the non-switched call (an M's scheduler context, or no M at all).
	shimLabels.place(ShimDirectLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: slotIndex)

	// done: patch the shim's own forward branches now that both labels' offsets are known.
	shimLabels.place(ShimDoneLabel, value: code.count())
	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: shimLabels, rel32Fixups: shimFixups)
end 'emitGtShimmedIatCall'

// --- Moving a green thread's stack: the GROWER, and the RELOCATION it shares with a shrink ---
//
// ⚠ A chain-walk bug here is a silent stack-corruption use-after-free.
//
// Nothing here rests on a machine count: a stack belongs to ONE GREEN THREAD, and it is moved either by that
// thread itself (a grow) or by the machine running its strand while it is parked (a shrink), never by both.
//
// SOUNDNESS: the spiller spills VALUES rsp-relative (never a stack pointer), and rbp is non-allocatable and used
// ONLY as the saved-rbp chain link. A frame ADDRESS (`StdOp.stackRecordAddr`) is never live where a stack moves:
// `promoteStackRecords` promotes nothing in a program that runs green threads, and the runtime functions that
// take their own frame scratch carry no prologue guard (so never grow) and never park. So the ONLY stack→stack
// pointers are (a) the saved-rbp chain links and (b) `gt.sp`/`gt.fp` in the heap GT struct; return addresses
// point into `.text` (unmoved). ⇒ Relocation = copy the live stack + rebase `gt.sp`/`gt.fp` + WALK the saved-rbp
// chain adding the relocation offset to each interior link — which is what frame-pointers-on-every-function pays
// for.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CHAIN STARTS AT `gt.fp` IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.** At a park, `__gt_context_switch` stores rbp in
// `gt.fp` and pushes only rbx/r12-r15, the XMMs and the return address — none of them a stack pointer — so the
// thread's live frame pointer lives in the struct and nowhere on the stack. `__gt_morestack` publishes its live
// rbp and entry rsp to `gt.fp`/`gt.sp` before relocating, so the grower's thread is presented in exactly the
// shape of a parked one.
//
// ENTRY CONTRACT (from a prologue guard, directly or through `__gt_guard_tripped`'s jump): r11 = the current GT,
// r10 = the system-stack top, r8 = the guard's PROJECTED rsp (all three non-arg scratch the guard loaded, so this
// chunk needs no `.data` of its own for them), rsp = the GT stack pointing at the return address the guard's
// `call` pushed. Everything it saves goes on the SYSTEM stack, so the tiny GT stack the guard is protecting never
// holds this frame, and it returns onto the RELOCATED stack.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT GROWS UNTIL THE FRAME THAT ASKED FITS, AS GO'S `newstack` DOES**, in one call: one doubling is not
// enough for a frame larger than the stack's free half. The projected rsp is what makes "fits" computable here —
// the new stack must hold everything above it plus the guard height below it — and the guard already has it in a
// register.
//
// ⭐⭐ WHAT IT PRESERVES, AND WHY THAT SET AND NOT ANOTHER. A function-entry guard is emitted at offset 0 of the
// entry block — BEFORE `push rbp` and before the `param` captures (X64Backend.emitFunctionChunk) — so the guarded
// function has established NOTHING yet: every incoming argument is still in its ABI register, and every
// callee-saved register still holds its CALLER's value. What must be saved is therefore
// (live at a function entry) ∩ (destroyed by this chunk or its callees):
//   • GPRs — `morestackSavedGprs`: rbp (the chain link), the five callee-saved this chunk's body works in, and the
//     six integer argument registers, which the page calls and `__gt_stack_relocate` take theirs in. r8, r10 and
//     r11 are the guard's scratch, so nothing is live in them.
//   • XMMs — the six FP ARGUMENT registers, `argFpRegisters` (xmm0-5). This chunk emits no SSE instruction at
//     all, but VirtualAlloc and VirtualFree are ordinary Win64 callees and xmm0-5 are Win64-VOLATILE, so they
//     come back destroyed. xmm6-15 need no save for the mirror-image reason: Win64 obliges kernel32 to preserve
//     them, which is the same guarantee `iatCall`'s clobber mask already rests on for every register-allocated
//     function in the program.
//
// `specs/async-scheduler.md`'s `a-float-argument-survives-a-stack-grow` /
// `every-fp-argument-register-survives-a-stack-grow` are the two RUNS that pin the XMM half — no golden fragment
// covers a hand-assembled chunk, and the sync form of the same program is correct because nothing grows a non-GT
// stack.

// The GPRs `__gt_morestack` pushes on the system stack, in push order. rbp is FIRST: its saved slot receives the
// rebased live rbp at a displacement derived from that position.
function morestackSavedGprs() returns X64RegisterArray
	var gprs = X64RegisterArray.create()
	gprs.push(X64Register.rbp)

	for reg in calleeSavedOrder 'calleeSaved'
		gprs.push(reg)
	end 'calleeSaved'

	for reg in argRegisters 'arguments'
		gprs.push(reg)
	end 'arguments'

	return gprs
end 'morestackSavedGprs'

// The ferried entry-rsp word sits one machine word below the system-stack top (`[systemStackTop - 8]`), and rsp
// is positioned there before the pushes begin.
let MorestackFerryDisp = -StackWordBytes as Imm32

// The `+8 rsp` that skips the ferried entry-rsp slot after the GPR pops, before switching to the new GT stack.
let MorestackFerrySkipBytes = StackWordBytes as Imm32

// The walk's own forward/backward branch targets, resolved against a WALK-LOCAL fixup list + offset map (the same
// machinery the shim and the block assembler use), scoped so the ids never collide with anything else.
let WalkHeadLabel = 0 as BlockId
let WalkDoneLabel = 1 as BlockId

// The guard's forward targets — its `skip` jumps, and past `mrt_panic`'s withdrawal of a request — in a
// GUARD-LOCAL map, which never collides with the enclosing function's block ids (the guard resolves against its
// own map before the function's blocks emit).
let GuardSkipLabel = 0 as BlockId
let GuardCompareLabel = 1 as BlockId

// A rebase's single forward skip target, in its OWN local map resolved at the end of each rebase — distinct from
// the walk's labels so the two scopes never read as one.
let RebaseSkipLabel = 0 as BlockId

// `__gt_guard_tripped`'s one forward target, the jump to the grower, in the chunk's own map.
let TrippedGrowLabel = 0 as BlockId

// The registers `__gt_morestack`'s body holds its persistent values in — all callee-saved, so the page call and
// `__gt_stack_relocate` preserve them, and all among `morestackSavedGprs`, so the guarded function gets back
// its caller's values.
let GtMoreGtReg = X64Register.rbx  // the current GT (moved from r11 after the pushes)
let GtMoreOldBaseReg = X64Register.r12  // old stack base
let GtMoreOldSizeReg = X64Register.r13  // old stack size
let GtMoreNewSizeReg = X64Register.r14  // new stack size = old * 2^k, the first that fits
let GtMoreNewBaseReg = X64Register.r15  // the bytes the new stack must hold, then its base (the allocation result)

// The guard's projected rsp — `__gt_morestack`'s third entry register. r8 is the internal ABI's primary RETURN
// register and no argument's, so nothing is live in it at a function entry.
let GtMorestackProjectedRspReg = X64Register.r8

// The growth loop's and the two refusals' targets, in the chunk's own GROW-LOCAL map: the refusals are placed
// after the `ret`, so this map is resolved last, after every other local map has been.
let GrowDoubleLabel = 0 as BlockId
let GrowExhaustedLabel = 1 as BlockId
let GrowRefusedLabel = 2 as BlockId

// The string-copy opcodes `__gt_stack_relocate`'s memcpy uses — `cld` (clear DF, so `movsb` ascends), then the
// `rep movsb` pair (F3 prefix + A4). Nothing else in the compiler emits a string op, so they live here rather than in the
// shared opcode table.
let OpcodeCld = 0xFC
let OpcodeRepPrefix = 0xF3
let OpcodeMovsb = 0xA4

// Which way a register block moves its registers.
enum GtRegisterBlockDirection
	save
	restore
end 'GtRegisterBlockDirection'

// ⭐ **SAVE OR RESTORE `gprs` AND EVERY FP ARGUMENT REGISTER, FROM ONE DESCRIPTION IN BOTH DIRECTIONS**, so a
// save and its restore cannot disagree about a slot — for a hand-assembled chunk the difference between a
// preserved argument and a wild one. The GPRs are pushed; below them the block reserves the Win64 shadow space
// its callees may clobber and, ABOVE that — the order `spillSlotBaseDisp` gives a compiled frame — one slot per FP
// argument register, padded so rsp is 16-aligned at the calls the block brackets. `stackedBytes` is what sits
// between the last 16-aligned rsp and the first push. Answers the bytes the block puts below them.
function emitGtRegisterBlock(code ByteArray, gprs X64RegisterArray, stackedBytes StackSize, direction GtRegisterBlockDirection) returns StackSize
	let pushedBytes = (gprs.count() * StackWordBytes) as StackSize
	let aboveFrame = stackedBytes + pushedBytes
	let frameBytes = alignUpStack(aboveFrame + ShadowSpaceBytes + (argFpRegisters.count() * XmmSaveSlotBytes), alignment: StackFrameAlignment) - aboveFrame

	if direction == GtRegisterBlockDirection.save 'save'
		for reg in gprs 'push'
			encodePushReg(code, reg: reg)
		end 'push'

		// Far inside a page, so it never probes; every caller holds the GT in r11.
		encodePrologue(code, stackSize: frameBytes, probeScratchIsFree: false)
		emitGtFpArgumentSlots(code, direction: direction)
	end 'save' else 'restore'
		emitGtFpArgumentSlots(code, direction: direction)
		encodeEpilogue(code, stackSize: frameBytes)

		for i in 0 upto gprs.count() 'pop'
			let reg = try gprs.get(gprs.count() - 1 - i) otherwise panic("emitGtRegisterBlock: pop index derived from count is in range")
			encodePopReg(code, reg: reg)
		end 'pop'
	end 'restore'

	return pushedBytes + frameBytes
end 'emitGtRegisterBlock'

// The FP argument registers' slots, `XmmSaveSlotBytes` apart above the shadow space.
function emitGtFpArgumentSlots(code ByteArray, direction GtRegisterBlockDirection)
	for k in 0 upto argFpRegisters.count() 'fps'
		let reg = try argFpRegisters.get(k) otherwise panic("emitGtFpArgumentSlots: index derived from count is in range")
		let disp = (ShadowSpaceBytes + ((k as RegCount) * XmmSaveSlotBytes)) as Imm32

		match direction 'dir'
			save then encodeMovsdBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: disp, srcReg: reg)
			restore then encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: reg, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: disp)
		end 'dir'
	end 'fps'
end 'emitGtFpArgumentSlots'

// `__gt_morestack` — grow the current GT's stack to the first doubling that fits the requesting frame, move the
// thread onto it (`__gt_stack_relocate`), store the stack marks of a grow (`GtStackMarksOfAGrow`), and `ret` onto
// the new (larger) stack so the frame whose guard called us fits. All of it runs on the system stack (entered r10
// = system-stack top), so the near-overflow GT stack never holds this frame. A growth past `GtMaxStackBytes` and a
// refused allocation both end the process.
function buildGtMorestackChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var iatRelocs = RelocationArray.create()
	var rdataRelocs = RdataRelocationArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()
	var growFixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var growLabels = BlockOffsetTable.create()
	let savedGprs = morestackSavedGprs()

	// Step 1: switch to the system stack, ferrying the GT entry rsp (rsp on entry — the guard's return-addr slot)
	// as the top word of the system-stack frame. `[r10 - 8] = entryRsp` then `rsp = r10 - 8`. r11 = gt and
	// r10 = systemStackTop both survive (neither the store, the lea, nor the pushes below touch them).
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: ShimGtRspReg, disp: MorestackFerryDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rsp)  // [r10-8] = entryRsp
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rsp, base: ShimGtRspReg, disp: MorestackFerryDisp)  // rsp = r10 - 8

	// Step 2: save what the ENTRY CONTRACT names. The system-stack top is 16-aligned, so the ferried word is all
	// that sits above the first push. Two words are addressed by displacement, both read off what the block laid
	// down: rbp's saved slot at the block's top, which receives the REBASED live rbp before the pops, and the
	// ferried ENTRY rsp just above it.
	let blockBytes = emitGtRegisterBlock(code, gprs: savedGprs, stackedBytes: StackWordBytes as StackSize, direction: GtRegisterBlockDirection.save)
	let savedRbpDisp = (blockBytes - StackWordBytes) as Imm32
	let ferriedRspDisp = blockBytes as Imm32

	// gt is still in r11 (nothing above clobbered it) — move it to rbx for the body.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtMoreGtReg, src: ShimGtReg, wide: true)  // rbx = gt

	// Step 3: repoint the TIB at the system stack for this routine's own page calls — VirtualAlloc here and the
	// VirtualFree inside `__gt_stack_relocate` (r10 = systemStackTop still). r10 is CONSUMED here: it is dead
	// until step 9 reloads it, so the shared helper may destroy it rather than spend a scratch register
	// preserving it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LINUX ARM HAS NO STEP 3 AT ALL**: the kernel probes no bounds pair, so `mmap` may be issued with rsp
	// wherever this chunk put it.
	if x64OsProbesStackBounds(target.os) 'repointForTheKernelCalls'
		emitRepointTibToSystemStack(code, topReg: ShimGtRspReg)  // gs:[0x08] = top ; gs:[0x10] = top - size
	end 'repointForTheKernelCalls'

	// Step 4: r12 = old_base ; r13 = old_size ; r15 = need = old_top - projected + guard height ; r14 = old_size
	// doubled until it holds `need`. A size past the ceiling is refused BEFORE anything is allocated — tested
	// ahead of the fit, so the loop ends even on a `need` no stack could hold.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: GtMoreOldBaseReg, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffStackBase as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: GtMoreOldSizeReg, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffStackSize as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewBaseReg, src: GtMoreOldBaseReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewBaseReg, src: GtMoreOldSizeReg, wide: true)  // r15 = old_top
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewBaseReg, src: GtMorestackProjectedRspReg, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: GtMoreNewBaseReg, imm: gtStackGuardHeight(target) as Imm32)  // r15 = need
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewSizeReg, src: GtMoreOldSizeReg, wide: true)

	growLabels.place(GrowDoubleLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewSizeReg, src: GtMoreNewSizeReg, wide: true)  // r14 += r14
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: GtMoreNewSizeReg, imm: GtMaxStackBytes as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: growFixups, cond: X64CondCode.above, targetBlockId: GrowExhaustedLabel)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewSizeReg, src: GtMoreNewBaseReg, wide: true)  // new_size - need
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: growFixups, cond: X64CondCode.below, targetBlockId: GrowDoubleLabel)

	// Step 5: new_base = <this OS's page source>(new_size) ; r15 = new_base. A refusal is NULL from `VirtualAlloc`
	// and a negative errno from a raw `mmap`, so the sign test catches both — `__slab_assert_os_alloc`'s test.
	emitGtMorestackAllocate(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, os: target.os)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: growFixups, cond: X64CondCode.lessEqual, targetBlockId: GrowRefusedLabel)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtMoreNewBaseReg, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)  // r15 = new_base

	// Step 6: present the thread where it stands — `gt.sp` = the entry rsp, `gt.fp` = the live rbp — and move it.
	// Writing the two words of a RUNNING thread is harmless: nothing reads them until its next switch-out
	// overwrites both.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: ferriedRspDisp, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffSp as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffFp as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rbp)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: GtMoreGtReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: GtMoreNewBaseReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: GtMoreNewSizeReg, wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtStackRelocateName)

	// Step 7: both stack marks (`GtStackMarksOfAGrow`) — the size was chosen now, and the thread has grown — and the
	// rebased live rbp goes to its saved slot so the pop below restores the relocated value.
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: GtStackMarksOfAGrow as MachineWord)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffStackMarks as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffFp as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: savedRbpDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	// Step 8: set the TIB to the NEW GT-stack bounds (`__gt_stack_relocate` wrote them to the struct) BEFORE we
	// pop rbx=gt. rcx is free scratch. After this, gs:[] describes the stack we return onto.
	if x64OsProbesStackBounds(target.os) 'restoreTheBounds'
		emitLoadTibFromGt(code, gtReg: GtMoreGtReg, scratch: X64Register.rcx)
	end 'restoreTheBounds'

	// Step 9: tear down + return onto the RELOCATED GT stack, at the rebased `gt.sp`, read while the GT is still in
	// rbx — the restore overwrites it. The FP argument registers come back after the last page call, which is the
	// call that can destroy them.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: ShimGtRspReg, base: GtMoreGtReg, disp: GtOffSp as Imm32, wide: true)  // r10 = relocated rsp
	_ = emitGtRegisterBlock(code, gprs: savedGprs, stackedBytes: StackWordBytes as StackSize, direction: GtRegisterBlockDirection.restore)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: X64Register.rsp, imm: MorestackFerrySkipBytes)  // add rsp, 8 (skip ferried slot)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsp, src: ShimGtRspReg, wide: true)  // rsp = relocated GT rsp
	code.push(OpcodeRet)  // ret into the copied return address, now on the new stack

	// The two refusals. rsp is on the system stack, 16-aligned, with the TIB describing it (step 3).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THEY GO THROUGH THE EXIT GATE LIKE EVERY OTHER EXIT (`usesGt: true`), AND AN ABORT IS EXACTLY THE
	// CASE THAT NEEDS IT.** The gate closes the monitor's suspend window before terminating; without it a
	// stack-exhaustion abort can run into a thread the monitor has suspended inside `ExitProcess`, which
	// hangs the process with nothing printed — the one deadlock the gate exists to prevent, reached by the
	// one path that is already reporting a fatal error. The gate carries the shim itself, and off a green
	// thread's own stack the shim takes its direct road anyway.
	growLabels.place(GrowExhaustedLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeX64ProcessExit(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, callFixups: callFixups, route: x64OsCallKind(target.os), exitCode: RuntimeAbort.greenThreadStackExhausted.rawValue as MachineWord, usesGt: true, target: target)
	growLabels.place(GrowRefusedLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeX64ProcessExit(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, callFixups: callFixups, route: x64OsCallKind(target.os), exitCode: RuntimeAbort.greenThreadStackAllocationFailed.rawValue as MachineWord, usesGt: true, target: target)
	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: growLabels, rel32Fixups: growFixups)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(GtMorestackName, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtMorestackChunk'

// The registers `__gt_stack_relocate` works in. The five persistent ones are callee-saved in the internal ABI, which
// is why the chunk pushes `calleeSavedOrder`; the rest are caller-saved. The walk's four are the ones
// `emitGtRelocateChainWalk` reads.
let RelocGtReg = X64Register.rbx
let RelocOldBaseReg = X64Register.r12
let RelocOldSizeReg = X64Register.r13
let RelocNewSizeReg = X64Register.r14
let RelocNewBaseReg = X64Register.r15
let RelocOffsetReg = X64Register.rax  // new_top - old_top
let RelocOldTopReg = X64Register.r10
let RelocNewLowReg = X64Register.r8  // the relocated `gt.sp`: the lowest live word on the new stack
let RelocNewHighReg = X64Register.r9  // new_top
let RelocWalkReg = X64Register.rcx
let RelocLinkReg = X64Register.r11

// ⭐⭐⭐ `__gt_stack_relocate(rcx = gt, rdx = newBase, rax = newSize)` — see `GtRuntime.GtStackRelocateName`.
// Called from `__gt_morestack` on the system stack, and from `__gt_stack_shrink` on a machine's scheduler
// context — an OS thread's own stack, whose TIB is its own — so the release's kernel call is direct either way.
//
// It copies `[gt.sp, old_top)` — only what is live — so the same routine can move a thread onto a SMALLER stack;
// the caller has established that the live region plus the guard height fits.
function buildGtStackRelocateChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var iatRelocs = RelocationArray.create()

	// rsp ≡ 8 on entry, so the pushes plus the shadow space must be a whole number of 16-byte units for the
	// release call to find rsp aligned.
	let stackedBytes = (1 + calleeSavedOrder.count()) * StackWordBytes

	if (stackedBytes + ShadowSpaceBytes) mod StackFrameAlignment != 0 'alignment'
		panic("__gt_stack_relocate misaligned: the return address + {calleeSavedOrder.count()} callee-saved pushes = {stackedBytes} bytes plus {ShadowSpaceBytes} of shadow space is not a multiple of {StackFrameAlignment} — rsp must be 16-aligned at the release call")
	end 'alignment'

	for reg in calleeSavedOrder 'push'
		encodePushReg(code, reg: reg)
	end 'push'

	encodePrologue(code, stackSize: ShadowSpaceBytes, probeScratchIsFree: false)

	// The arguments into their homes; rdx and rax are read before rcx's value moves on.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocGtReg, src: X64Register.rcx, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocNewBaseReg, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocNewSizeReg, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: RelocOldBaseReg, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffStackBase as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: RelocOldSizeReg, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffStackSize as Imm32, wide: true)

	// old_top, new_top, and offset = new_top - old_top: every address in the live region moves by that one amount,
	// because the region is copied to the TOP of the new stack.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocOldTopReg, src: RelocOldBaseReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: RelocOldTopReg, src: RelocOldSizeReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocNewHighReg, src: RelocNewBaseReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: RelocNewHighReg, src: RelocNewSizeReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocOffsetReg, src: RelocNewHighReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: RelocOffsetReg, src: RelocOldTopReg, wide: true)

	// memcpy [gt.sp, old_top) → [gt.sp + offset, new_top): rsi = gt.sp, rcx = the live bytes, rdi = new_low, which
	// is also the rebased `gt.sp`.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rsi, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffSp as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: RelocOldTopReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rsi, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocNewLowReg, src: X64Register.rsi, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: RelocNewLowReg, src: RelocOffsetReg, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdi, src: RelocNewLowReg, wide: true)
	code.push(OpcodeCld)
	code.push(OpcodeRepPrefix)
	code.push(OpcodeMovsb)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffSp as Imm32, srcReg: RelocNewLowReg)

	// `gt.fp` rebased in place, then the chain from it.
	emitGtRelocateRebaseWord(code, reg: RelocWalkReg, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffFp as Imm32)
	emitGtRelocateChainWalk(code)

	// The GT's own view of its stack: base, size, the guard word, and — where the kernel probes it — the TIB pair
	// the next switch-in (or `__gt_morestack`'s step 8) loads.
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffStackBase as Imm32, srcReg: RelocNewBaseReg)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffStackSize as Imm32, srcReg: RelocNewSizeReg)

	// ⚠ A preemption request `__sysmon` stored into the guard word meanwhile is overwritten here and lost; it is
	// sent again at the next lap while the strand still holds its processor, a loss Go accepts at the same store
	// (`stack.go:999`).
	emitGtGuardWordRestore(code, gt: RelocGtReg, scratch: X64Register.rcx, target: target)

	if x64OsProbesStackBounds(target.os) 'republishTheBounds'
		encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffTibStackBase as Imm32, srcReg: RelocNewHighReg)
		encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: RelocGtReg, disp: GtOffTibStackLimit as Imm32, srcReg: RelocNewBaseReg)
	end 'republishTheBounds'

	// Give the old stack back. Every pointer into it has been rewritten, so nothing that survives this can read it.
	emitGtStackRelease(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, os: target.os)

	encodeEpilogue(code, stackSize: ShadowSpaceBytes)

	for i in 0 upto calleeSavedOrder.count() 'pop'
		let reg = try calleeSavedOrder.get(calleeSavedOrder.count() - 1 - i) otherwise panic("buildGtStackRelocateChunk: pop index derived from count is in range")
		encodePopReg(code, reg: reg)
	end 'pop'

	code.push(OpcodeRet)
	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(GtStackRelocateName, code: code, callFixups: CallFixupArray.create(), rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(), iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtStackRelocateChunk'

// The guard word of the GT in `gt` set to its stack's base plus the guard height — where every stack the thread
// runs on starts it.
function emitGtGuardWordRestore(code ByteArray, gt X64Register, scratch X64Register, target Target)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: scratch, base: gt, disp: GtOffStackBase as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: scratch, imm: gtStackGuardHeight(target) as Imm32)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: gt, disp: GtOffStackGuard as Imm32, srcReg: scratch)
end 'emitGtGuardWordRestore'

// `reg += offset` IFF `reg` points inside the OLD stack `[old_base, old_top)` — the one statement of that test, for
// `gt.fp` and for every chain link, as `Arm64GtRuntime.emitArm64RelocateRebase` is on the other lane. UNSIGNED
// compares: these are addresses.
function emitGtRelocateRebase(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	var fixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var labels = BlockOffsetTable.create()

	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: reg, src: RelocOldBaseReg, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.below, targetBlockId: RebaseSkipLabel)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: reg, src: RelocOldTopReg, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetBlockId: RebaseSkipLabel)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: reg, src: RelocOffsetReg, wide: true)

	labels.place(RebaseSkipLabel, value: code.count())
	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: labels, rel32Fixups: fixups)
end 'emitGtRelocateRebase'

// The word at `[base + disp]` rebased in place, left in `reg` for a caller that follows it.
function emitGtRelocateRebaseWord(code ByteArray, reg X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: reg, base: base, disp: disp, wide: true)
	emitGtRelocateRebase(code, reg: reg)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: base, disp: disp, srcReg: reg)
end 'emitGtRelocateRebaseWord'

// The saved-rbp chain walk — the correctness crux. Entered with the walker (the rebased `gt.fp`) in `RelocWalkReg`.
// For each frame whose saved rbp points into the old stack it adds the relocation offset, then follows the
// now-adjusted link. It ends when the walker leaves the relocated region `[new_low, new_high)` — which a null
// link does too: a green thread's outermost frame is the trampoline's, whose saved rbp is the seed zero.
function emitGtRelocateChainWalk(code ByteArray)
	var walkFixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var walkLabels = BlockOffsetTable.create()

	walkLabels.place(WalkHeadLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: RelocWalkReg, src: RelocNewLowReg, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: walkFixups, cond: X64CondCode.below, targetBlockId: WalkDoneLabel)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: RelocWalkReg, src: RelocNewHighReg, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: walkFixups, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetBlockId: WalkDoneLabel)
	emitGtRelocateRebaseWord(code, reg: RelocLinkReg, base: RelocWalkReg, disp: 0)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: RelocWalkReg, src: RelocLinkReg, wide: true)
	encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: walkFixups, targetBlockId: WalkHeadLabel)

	walkLabels.place(WalkDoneLabel, value: code.count())
	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: walkLabels, rel32Fixups: walkFixups)
end 'emitGtRelocateChainWalk'

// ⭐ The prologue stack GUARD. X64Backend emits it at the very START of a guarded function's entry block — BEFORE
// `push rbp` — iff the module carries the GT scheduler, the function is NOT a runtime (`__`/`mrt_`) function, and
// it reserves a frame. When the running GT's projected rsp would dip below its guard word the cold path runs;
// otherwise it is a handful of instructions that fall through. A no-op for an M's scheduler context (guard word 0
// ⇒ `jae` always taken) and off a green thread (current GT 0 ⇒ bail). It preserves every register but r8, r10
// and r11 — all volatile, and none an argument's — and the flags.
//
// ⭐ **AT A FUNCTION ENTRY THE COLD PATH CALLS `__gt_guard_tripped` AND JUMPS BACK TO THE GUARD's FIRST BYTE —
// Go's `morestack` re-entering the function.** A preemption request (`GtRuntime.GtStackGuardPreempt`) fails this
// compare exactly as an overflow does, and is answered without growing; the guard then runs again on whatever
// stack the park left the thread, and a request that hid a real overflow grows on the second pass.
//
// ⛔ **`mrt_panic`'s GUARD NEVER PARKS** (`GtGuardSite.mrtPanic`): it withdraws a request before its compare and
// calls `__gt_morestack` itself, with no jump back, because the grower makes the frame fit in one call. A request
// `__sysmon` stores after the withdrawal costs one needless grow.
export function emitGtStackGuard(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, frameBytes StackSize, margin ParsedInt, site GtGuardSite, target Target)
	var guardFixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var guardLabels = BlockOffsetTable.create()
	let guardTop = code.count()

	// r11 = this OS thread's green thread; no scheduler, no M and no current GT all mean "not on a green
	// thread" → skip. The M is dead the moment it has answered, so it shares r11 with the GT.
	emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, fixups: guardFixups, mDest: ShimGtReg, gtDest: ShimGtReg, bail: GuardSkipLabel, os: target.os)

	if site == GtGuardSite.mrtPanic 'withdrawARequest'
		emitGtBranchUnlessPreemptRequest(code, fixups: guardFixups, unlessRequested: GuardCompareLabel)
		emitGtGuardWordRestore(code, gt: ShimGtReg, scratch: ShimGtRspReg, target: target)
		guardLabels.place(GuardCompareLabel, value: code.count())
	end 'withdrawARequest'

	// projectedRsp (r10) = rsp - (frameBytes + margin) ; enough room (>= stackGuard) → skip.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: ShimGtRspReg, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: ShimGtRspReg, imm: (frameBytes + (margin as StackSize)) as Imm32)
	encodeCmpRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: ShimGtRspReg, base: ShimGtReg, disp: GtOffStackGuard as Imm32)  // projected - stackGuard
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: guardFixups, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetBlockId: GuardSkipLabel)

	// Cold path: r8 = the projected rsp, r10 = THIS M's system-stack top (the scratch stack `__gt_morestack` runs
	// on), r11 = the current GT — the ENTRY CONTRACT of both callees. ⚠ The M is re-read rather than kept from the
	// guard above, because r11 has since been repurposed to hold the GT, and the guard's hot path must not spend a
	// second register to save a load on its cold one.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: GtMorestackProjectedRspReg, src: ShimGtRspReg, wide: true)
	emitLoadCurrentMOrBailless(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: ShimGtRspReg, os: target.os)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: ShimGtRspReg, base: ShimGtRspReg, disp: MOffSystemStackSP as Imm32, wide: true)

	if site == GtGuardSite.functionEntry 'mayPark'
		encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtGuardTrippedName)
		encodeJmpBackwardShort(code, targetOffset: guardTop)
	end 'mayPark' else 'mustNotPark'
		encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtMorestackName)
	end 'mustNotPark'

	// skip:
	guardLabels.place(GuardSkipLabel, value: code.count())
	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: guardLabels, rel32Fixups: guardFixups)
end 'emitGtStackGuard'

// Branch to `unlessRequested` when the guard word of the GT in r11 holds anything but a preemption request.
function emitGtBranchUnlessPreemptRequest(code ByteArray, fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, unlessRequested BlockId)
	encodeGrp1BaseDispImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, base: ShimGtReg, disp: GtOffStackGuard as Imm32, imm: GtStackGuardPreempt as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: unlessRequested)
end 'emitGtBranchUnlessPreemptRequest'

// `__gt_guard_tripped` — see `GtRuntime.GtGuardTrippedName`. Entered with `__gt_morestack`'s ENTRY CONTRACT, which
// it hands on untouched by jumping when the guard word holds no request.
function buildGtGuardTrippedChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()
	var fixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var labels = BlockOffsetTable.create()

	emitGtBranchUnlessPreemptRequest(code, fixups: fixups, unlessRequested: TrippedGrowLabel)
	_ = emitGtPreemptArm(code, callFixups: callFixups)

	labels.place(TrippedGrowLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeJmpDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtMorestackName)
	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: labels, rel32Fixups: fixups)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(GtGuardTrippedName, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(), iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(), rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtGuardTrippedChunk'

// ⭐⭐ **THE PREEMPT ARM: A REQUEST IS HONOURED ON THE GREEN THREAD's OWN STACK — Go's `newstack` preempt arm**,
// because the thread must park where it stands: it may resume on another machine. It is reached from a
// function-entry guard only, so the guarded function has established nothing: every incoming argument is still in
// its register and every callee-saved register still holds the caller's value. The arm saves the argument
// registers — `argRegisters` and `argFpRegisters`, the backend's own tables — and `__gt_preempt`, an ordinary
// callee, preserves the rest. It returns to the guard, whose jump back re-runs the whole check.
//
// ⚠ **IT ALIGNS rsp ITSELF**, so its calls are aligned whatever parity it is entered at. The frame pointer it
// pushes to undo that keeps the saved-rbp chain whole, so a stack moved while the thread is parked
// (`__gt_stack_relocate`) rebases this frame's link and `mov rsp, rbp` lands on the relocated stack.
//
// It answers what it puts on the stack below its return address: the pushed rbp, the most `and rsp` can drop from
// a word-aligned rsp, and its register block — all of it before `__gt_preempt` runs.
function emitGtPreemptArm(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray) returns StackSize
	encodePushReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rbp, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAnd, reg: X64Register.rsp, imm: (0 - StackFrameAlignment) as Imm32)
	let blockBytes = emitGtRegisterBlock(code, gprs: argRegisters, stackedBytes: 0, direction: GtRegisterBlockDirection.save)

	// `__gt_preempt(gt)`: the GT into the first argument register, whose own value is already saved.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: argRegisterAt(0), src: ShimGtReg, wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtPreemptName)

	_ = emitGtRegisterBlock(code, gprs: argRegisters, stackedBytes: 0, direction: GtRegisterBlockDirection.restore)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsp, src: X64Register.rbp, wide: true)
	encodePopReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
	code.push(OpcodeRet)

	return (StackWordBytes + (StackFrameAlignment - StackWordBytes)) as StackSize + blockBytes
end 'emitGtPreemptArm'

// ⭐ **WHAT A HONOURED PREEMPTION REQUEST COSTS THE GREEN THREAD's STACK BELOW THE GUARD, BEFORE `__gt_preempt`
// RUNS**: the guard's `call` return address and the arm, read off the arm's own emitter.
// `requirePreemptionFitsTheGuardMargin` adds `__gt_preempt`'s tree to it.
export function x64GtPreemptArmStackBytes() returns StackSize
	return StackWordBytes as StackSize + emitGtPreemptArm(ByteArray.create(), callFixups: CallFixupArray.create())
end 'x64GtPreemptArmStackBytes'

// What a shimmed import call adds below its caller's frame on a green thread's own stack. The kernel call
// itself runs on the machine's system stack, so an unbracketed slot costs the one word the shim pushes; a
// bracketed one may also take its slow exit, which opens a frame and calls `__sched_syscall_regain` on this
// stack after that word is popped.
export function x64ShimmedImportStep(slotIndex RelocSlotIndex) returns UnguardedCallStep
	if gtShimSyscallClassOf(iatSlotOfIndex(slotIndex)) == SyscallClass.blocking 'bracketed'
		return UnguardedCallStep.callee(SchedSyscallRegainName, below: exitSyscallSlowFrameBytes())
	end 'bracketed'

	return UnguardedCallStep.fixed(ShimPushedWordBytes)
end 'x64ShimmedImportStep'

// Append the hand-assembled GT chunks — called by BackendDispatch when the module spawns a green thread,
// alongside `appendX64RuntimeChunks` for the panic runtime. They lay out above user code like every other
// runtime chunk, so `mrt_start` stays at `.text` offset 0.

// --- The IOCP completion-drain thread (P1.5 slice 1a-ii) ---
//
// ⭐ A HAND-ASSEMBLED chunk, NOT builder-built Std — like `__gt_context_switch`/`__gt_trampoline`/`__gt_morestack`,
// and for the same reason: it runs OUTSIDE the GT model, on its OWN OS-thread stack, so its kernel calls must be
// DIRECT. A builder-built version would be shimmed and stack-guarded — both wrong here.
//
// `__io_completion_loop(rcx = lpParameter, ignored)` — the Win64 ThreadProc `__io_init` starts with CreateThread.
// It loops: `GetQueuedCompletionStatus` blocks until an overlapped read completes; the completing packet's
// lpOverlapped IS the reading GT's embedded OVERLAPPED, so `gt = lpOverlapped - GtOffOverlapped` (CONTAINING_RECORD).
// It writes to `gt.io_result` the byte count, or `SubpReadFailedCount` when the packet says the IO FAILED for some
// reason other than the broken pipe that is a clean end of stream. Then — THE HANDSHAKE — it SPIN-WAITS, holding no
// lock, until `gt.ioParked` is non-zero (the reading GT's registration sets it only AFTER `status = waiting`, so the
// ready below lands after the GT's own waiting-write: no torn status, no lost wakeup). It readies the GT under
// `__sched_lock` (`__gt_ready_locked`) and pays the wake debt that answers after the release. The ready door is PURE
// MEMORY (no shim), so calling it from this thread is safe.
//
// gt is spilled to a frame local (not a callee-saved register) and reloaded after each call, which
// keeps the frame layout — and the 16-byte alignment at every `call` — trivial.

// Frame locals (rbp-relative) and the GQCS 5th-argument stack slot (rsp-relative). The frame is 0x50 bytes: with the
// `push rbp` it lands RSP 16-aligned at every `call` (entry RSP ≡ 8 mod 16; push rbp ⇒ 0; sub 0x50 ⇒ 0).
let IoLoopFrameBytes = 0x50 as StackSize
let IoLoopBytesLocalDisp = -0x08 as Imm32  // lpNumberOfBytesTransferred out-param
let IoLoopKeyLocalDisp = -0x10 as Imm32  // lpCompletionKey out-param (unused)
let IoLoopOverlappedLocalDisp = -0x18 as Imm32  // lpOverlapped out-param → the completing GT's OVERLAPPED
let IoLoopGtLocalDisp = -0x20 as Imm32  // the recovered GT pointer, spilled across the Win32/enqueue calls

// ⭐ **THE WAKE DEBT `__gt_ready_locked` ANSWERED** — `WakeDebtOwed` when a strand token was published, so a
// `__sched_wake_or_spawn` is owed after the release. It is a FRAME LOCAL rather than a register because
// `LeaveCriticalSection` sits between the answer and its use and clobbers every volatile register.
//
// ⚠ rbp-0x28 is rsp+0x28 in this 0x50 frame, one slot above the outgoing-argument region
// (`win64OutgoingArgDisp(0)` = rsp+0x20, eight bytes wide), so it collides with nothing.
let IoLoopWakeLocalDisp = -0x28 as Imm32
let IoLoopInfiniteTimeout = 0xFFFFFFFF  // INFINITE

// The completion loop's own forward/backward branch targets, resolved against a LOOP-LOCAL fixup list + offset map
// (the same machinery the shim and the block assembler use), scoped so the ids never collide with anything else.
let IoLoopTopLabel = 0 as BlockId
let IoLoopSpinLabel = 1 as BlockId
let IoLoopSpunLabel = 2 as BlockId
let IoLoopExitLabel = 4 as BlockId
let IoLoopAbandonedLabel = 5 as BlockId
let IoLoopCleanEofLabel = 7 as BlockId
let IoLoopDequeuedLabel = 8 as BlockId

// The completion loop's scratch: r11 shuttles a `.data` global's address, rax/rcx are general scratch. None is read
// across the loop's own iterations (gt lives in the frame), so any caller-saved use is invisible.
let IoLoopGlobalPtrReg = X64Register.r11

// Load a `.data` global's VALUE into `dest` — `lea r11, [rip+label] ; mov dest, [r11]`. The completion loop reads
// `__io_iocp`/`__sched_lock` this way, through the ordinary rdata-relocation path.
function emitIoLoopLoadGlobal(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, dest X64Register, label ByteArray)
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: IoLoopGlobalPtrReg, label: label)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: dest, base: IoLoopGlobalPtrReg, disp: 0, wide: true)
end 'emitIoLoopLoadGlobal'

function buildIoCompletionLoopChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()
	var iatRelocs = RelocationArray.create()
	var rdataRelocs = RdataRelocationArray.create()
	var loopFixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	var loopLabels = BlockOffsetTable.create()

	// Prologue: push rbp ; mov rbp, rsp ; sub rsp, 0x50.
	encodePushReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rbp, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: X64Register.rsp, imm: IoLoopFrameBytes as Imm32)

	// loop_top:
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopTopLabel, value: code.count())

	// Zero the bytes slot (GQCS writes only the low DWORD; the runtime reads a full i64).
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: 0)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopBytesLocalDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// GetQueuedCompletionStatus(iocp, &bytes, &key, &overlapped, INFINITE).
	emitIoLoopLoadGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.rcx, label: IoIocpLabel)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopBytesLocalDisp)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.r8, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopKeyLocalDisp)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopOverlappedLocalDisp)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: IoLoopInfiniteTimeout)

	// `GetQueuedCompletionStatus`'s 5th argument (dwMilliseconds) goes in outgoing stack SLOT 0 of this loop's
	// own frame, so the displacement is `win64OutgoingArgDisp(0)` — the SAME rule the ISEL and the GT shim place
	// their stack arguments by. Asked HERE rather than bound to a global `let`, which may hold no function call.
	// This is the one place that writes the stack argument `GetQueuedCompletionStatusStackArgWords` counts.
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(0), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetQueuedCompletionStatus))

	// ⭐⭐ **A COMPLETION CAN CARRY A FAILURE, AND THE BYTE-COUNT SLOT IS WHERE IT TRAVELS.** A FALSE with a
	// NON-NULL `lpOverlapped` is the PARKED twin of the synchronous `ReadFile` refusal `__gt_subp_yield_read`
	// classifies — and parking is the normal path for every stdlib reader, so a `readFailed` the enum promises
	// would otherwise be a state only the rare synchronous path could reach. The verdict rides the SAME `bytes`
	// slot a count does, which is what leaves the `gt.io_result` store below with one path and the resumed
	// reader with one word to read.
	//
	// ⚠ `GetLastError` is called with NOTHING between it and the failing call — that is the whole of its contract.
	// ⚠ A FALSE with a NULL `lpOverlapped` means the WAIT failed rather than an IO; the exit test below takes that
	// path and the slot written here is never read.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: IoLoopDequeuedLabel)
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetLastError))

	// Widened through a 32-bit register move because the Win64 ABI leaves RAX's high half undefined for a DWORD
	// return, and the 64-bit compare below reads the whole register.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rax, wide: false)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.rcx, imm: ErrorBrokenPipe as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: IoLoopCleanEofLabel)

	// `or reg, imm` rather than a `mov`: this immediate is SIGN-EXTENDED from the signed `Imm32` domain the
	// constant lives in, where `encodeMovRegImm64` takes an unsigned `MachineWord` and could not carry it.
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtOr, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: SubpReadFailedCount as Imm32)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopBytesLocalDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, targetBlockId: IoLoopDequeuedLabel)

	// clean_eof: the child closed its stdout. The 0 is stored rather than left to what the API put in the
	// out-parameter, so the answer does not rest on a field documented only as "may be zero".
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopCleanEofLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: 0)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopBytesLocalDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// dequeued: the bytes slot holds the count, 0 for a clean end of stream, or the failure marker.
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopDequeuedLabel, value: code.count())

	// overlapped == NULL → shutdown sentinel → exit (defensive; the slice never posts one).
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopOverlappedLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: IoLoopExitLabel)

	// gt = overlapped - GtOffOverlapped ; spill to the frame.
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: GtOffOverlapped as Imm32)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopGtLocalDisp, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// spin: wait until gt.ioParked != 0 — the reader's registration has published its park (IoParkedInFlight, 1),
	// or the drop arm has abandoned it (IoParkedAbandoned, 2). The packet can arrive before the reader has even
	// switched out, and everything between its `ReadFile` and that registration — the syscall bracket's exit, which
	// may wait on `__sched_lock` for a processor, among it — must be able to run, so the spin holds no lock.
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopSpinLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopGtLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rax, disp: GtOffIoParked as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeTestRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rcx, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: IoLoopSpunLabel)
	code.push(0xF3)  // PAUSE (F3 90) — spin hint, reduces power and HT contention.
	code.push(0x90)
	encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, targetBlockId: IoLoopSpinLabel)

	// ⭐ spun: LOCK __sched_lock, held across the WHOLE decision below — so the completion thread's
	// abandoned-vs-normal branch and the `__gt_promise_drop` IO arm's in-flight-vs-already-fired branch are
	// SERIALIZED, guaranteeing exactly one side owns the GT. `ioParked` is re-read under it: the drop arm may have
	// moved it from 1 to 2 since the spin read it, and nothing but this thread moves it on from either.
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopSpunLabel, value: code.count())
	emitIoLoopLoadGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.rcx, label: SchedLockLabel)
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.EnterCriticalSection))
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopGtLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rax, disp: GtOffIoParked as Imm32, wide: true)

	// rax = gt, rcx = gt.ioParked. ABANDONED (2) → the drop arm cancelled this read: signal drain-done and touch the
	// GT no further (the drop arm frees it). Else (1) → NORMAL completion.
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.rcx, imm: IoParkedAbandoned as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: IoLoopAbandonedLabel)

	// NORMAL: gt.io_result = the classified count.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopBytesLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rax, disp: GtOffIoResult as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	// Ready the reader under the outer __sched_lock — exactly the lock `__gt_ready_locked` requires of its
	// caller. The door reads which strand the reader belongs to off the GT itself: this thread has no M, so
	// nothing here could say. A strand token it publishes goes to the GLOBAL queue (`__sched_runq_put`'s
	// `p == 0` arm), the tier a token belongs to when no processor owns it.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ITS ANSWER IS THE WAKE DEBT, WHICH IS WHY THE RESULT IS SPILLED RATHER THAN DISCARDED.** A token
	// published here has no machine looking for it, so somebody owes `__sched_wake_or_spawn`. It may not be
	// called here: the caller holds the lock, and a wake inside a critical section has no StoreLoad fence (the
	// release IS the fence). It is paid below, after `LeaveCriticalSection`, and ONLY if owed — a member readied
	// onto a strand some machine is already running owes nobody, and an unconditional wake would start an OS
	// thread that finds nothing to run.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopGtLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtReadyLockedName)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopWakeLocalDisp, srcReg: X64Register.r8)

	// gt.ioParked = IoParkedNormalDone(4) — so a drop RIGHT AFTER this completion reads 4 and knows the GT is
	// queued rather than in flight — then Leave __sched_lock. ⚠ It does NOT unlink: a queue is not spliced,
	// so the drop renounces the GT in place, leaving a tombstone the queue's popper reclaims.
	// `emitGtIoCancelArm` carries the protocol.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopGtLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: IoParkedNormalDone)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rax, disp: GtOffIoParked as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	emitIoLoopLoadGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.rcx, label: SchedLockLabel)
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.LeaveCriticalSection))

	// ⭐⭐ **PAY THE WAKE DEBT, AFTER THE UNLOCK — AND THAT ORDER IS THE DEKKER ORDERING RATHER THAN LOCK
	// HYGIENE.** `LeaveCriticalSection` is a full barrier, so the queue publish above cannot be reordered past
	// `__sched_wake_or_spawn`'s reads of every P's `idleFlag`; called inside the critical section it would have
	// no fence and a missed wakeup would be a token waiting out a parked machine's timeout.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopWakeLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: WakeDebtNone as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: IoLoopTopLabel)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: SchedWakeOrSpawnName)
	encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, targetBlockId: IoLoopTopLabel)

	// abandoned: gt.ioParked = IoParkedDrainDone(3) (the drop arm's spin target), Leave __sched_lock, loop. No ready
	// and no wake — the drop arm spins on ioParked==3 on the dropping thread and owns the free.
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopAbandonedLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: IoLoopGtLocalDisp, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: IoParkedDrainDone)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rax, disp: GtOffIoParked as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	emitIoLoopLoadGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.rcx, label: SchedLockLabel)
	encodeIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.LeaveCriticalSection))
	encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: loopFixups, targetBlockId: IoLoopTopLabel)

	// exit: return 0.
	loopLabels.place(IoLoopExitLabel, value: code.count())
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: 0)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: X64Register.rsp, imm: IoLoopFrameBytes as Imm32)
	encodePopReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
	code.push(OpcodeRet)

	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: loopLabels, rel32Fixups: loopFixups)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(IoCompletionLoopName, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildIoCompletionLoopChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ ASYNCHRONOUS PREEMPTION — STOPPING A THREAD THAT REACHES NO PROLOGUE (x64-windows)
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE COOPERATIVE PATH STAYS AND THIS IS ITS BACKSTOP.** `__sched_preemptone` poisons the running
// thread's guard word, and a function that reserves a frame reads the poison at its prologue. A loop that
// calls nothing never reaches one — so the monitor takes the thread's register file away from it, pushes the
// interrupted PC on the thread's own stack and hands the file back with RIP pointing at `__gt_async_preempt`.
// The thread resumes INTO the trampoline, spills everything, parks, and later returns to the instruction it
// was executing.
//
// ⛔ **THE POISON IS STILL WHAT AUTHORISES THE STOP** (`SchedRuntime.buildGtPreemptSafe` tests for it), so an
// asynchronous stop is never a thing that happens to a thread nobody asked to yield.

// The rights a machine opens onto its OWN thread at birth: suspend it, read its register file, write it back.
// Exactly the three things `__gt_preempt_m` does.
let ThreadSuspendResume = 0x0002
let ThreadGetContext = 0x0008
let ThreadSetContext = 0x0010
let PreemptThreadAccess = ThreadSuspendResume or ThreadGetContext or ThreadSetContext

// `SuspendThread` answers the thread's previous suspend count, or `(DWORD)-1` if the thread is gone. The
// answer is a DWORD, so the failure value is compared as one.
let SuspendThreadFailed = 0xFFFFFFFF

// Win32's FALSE: what `OpenThread` is handed for `bInheritHandle`, and what `GetThreadContext` /
// `SetThreadContext` answer on failure.
let PreemptWin32False = 0

// `lock cmpxchg r/m64, r64` (F0 0F B1 /r) and `lock xadd r/m64, r64` (F0 0F C1 /r) — the two-byte-escape
// opcodes the claims and the acknowledgement counter ride.
let OpcodeCmpxchg2ndByte = 0xB1
let OpcodeXadd2ndByte = 0xC1

// `pushfq` / `popfq`: the only way to carry the interrupted thread's flags across the park, and the reason
// the trampoline saves them BEFORE it aligns its stack (`and` writes flags).
let OpcodePushfq = 0x9C
let OpcodePopfq = 0x9D

// `pause` — the spin hint in `__gt_exit_process`'s wait for the monitor's window to close.
let OpcodePauseRepPrefix = 0xF3
let OpcodePauseNop = 0x90

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY GENERAL REGISTER THE TRAMPOLINE SPILLS — all of them but rsp and rbp.** The stop lands at an
// arbitrary instruction, so every one of these may hold a live value of the interrupted function; rsp is
// restored by the frame arithmetic and rbp by the `push` that opens it.
//
// ⛔ **IT IS A HAND-WRITTEN LIST AND `assertAsyncPreemptGprOrder` IS WHAT KEEPS IT HONEST.** A register
// missing from it is one the interrupted function silently loses — no fault, no diagnostic, a wrong answer
// wherever that register happened to be live. Its XMM sibling is derived from the two files that partition
// the vector registers; the GPR half cannot be, because the two registers it omits are omitted for
// different reasons, so the COUNT is checked against the register file instead.
let AsyncPreemptGprOrder = [X64Register.rax, X64Register.rcx, X64Register.rdx, X64Register.rbx, X64Register.rsi, X64Register.rdi, X64Register.r8, X64Register.r9, X64Register.r10, X64Register.r11, X64Register.r12, X64Register.r13, X64Register.r14, X64Register.r15]

// The two the list leaves out: rsp, which the frame arithmetic restores, and rbp, which the `push` that
// opens the frame does.
let AsyncPreemptUnspilledGprs = 2 as RegCount

function assertAsyncPreemptGprOrder()
	let file = x64RegisterFile()
	let expected = (file.firstVector as RegCount) - AsyncPreemptUnspilledGprs

	if AsyncPreemptGprOrder.count() != expected 'incomplete'
		panic("assertAsyncPreemptGprOrder: `__gt_async_preempt` spills {AsyncPreemptGprOrder.count()} general registers but the x64 file has {file.firstVector} of them and the frame restores {AsyncPreemptUnspilledGprs} — a stop lands at an arbitrary instruction, so every register missing from that list is one the interrupted function loses with no fault and no diagnostic")
	end 'incomplete'
end 'assertAsyncPreemptGprOrder'

// The chunk-local labels of the two Windows chunks.
let PreemptGiveBackLabel = 0 as BlockId
let PreemptUnlockLabel = 1 as BlockId
let PreemptResumeLabel = 2 as BlockId
let PreemptDoneLabel = 3 as BlockId
let PreemptNoMachineLabel = 4 as BlockId
let ExitProcessSpinLabel = 0 as BlockId

// ⭐⭐ **THE INJECTION, WRITTEN ONCE FOR BOTH WAYS A STOP IS DELIVERED.** `sp -= 8; [sp] = pc;
// pc = __gt_async_preempt`, applied to a SAVED register file — a Windows `CONTEXT` the monitor read out of a
// suspended thread, or the `ucontext` the kernel handed a signal handler. The two differ in where the file
// lives and in the record's two displacements; nothing else about the act differs, which is why those are
// the parameters. It leaves the file's address in r10, which the Windows caller hands straight to
// `SetThreadContext`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE WORD GOES BELOW THE INTERRUPTED STACK POINTER, WHICH IS FREE ONLY BECAUSE THIS ABI HAS NO RED
// ZONE** — `X64PrologueEpilogue.X64RedZoneBytes`. The refusal lives HERE, at the one site both lanes come
// through, and not on the Windows half alone: x86-64 System V is the ABI that HAS a red zone, so Linux is
// the lane a non-zero one would reach first.
function emitX64PreemptInject(asm X64RuntimeAsm, fileSlot StackSize, spOffset Imm32, pcOffset Imm32)
	if X64RedZoneBytes != 0 'aRedZone'
		panic("emitX64PreemptInject: this ABI declares a {X64RedZoneBytes}-byte red zone below RSP, and an asynchronous stop writes the interrupted PC there — the injected word would overwrite live data of the interrupted function at every stop, silently. Push the PC below the red zone and teach `{String.from(AsyncPreemptName)}` to restore RSP past it")
	end 'aRedZone'

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(fileSlot), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.r10, disp: spOffset, wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: -(StackWordBytes as Imm32))
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.r10, disp: pcOffset, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rax, disp: 0, srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: spOffset, srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, dest: X64Register.rdx, targetName: AsyncPreemptName)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: pcOffset, srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
end 'emitX64PreemptInject'

// CAS(m.signalPending, free → held) on the machine in `m`, branching to `alreadyOutstanding` when a stop is
// already in flight against it. Clobbers rax and rdx, which every caller has just finished with.
function emitX64PreemptClaim(asm X64RuntimeAsm, m X64Register, alreadyOutstanding BlockId)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: PreemptClaimFree)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: PreemptClaimHeld)
	encodeLockedRmwBaseDispReg(asm.code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeCmpxchg2ndByte, base: m, disp: MOffSignalPending as Imm32, reg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: alreadyOutstanding)
end 'emitX64PreemptClaim'

// ⛔ **HAND THE CLAIM BACK — EVERY ROAD THAT DOES NOT INJECT OWES THIS.** A machine left claimed is a machine
// the monitor never stops again for the life of the process, which is the one failure mode of this word that
// is silent. Reads the machine back out of `machineSlot` rather than a register, because the roads that reach
// it have spent every scratch register on the kernel calls in between.
function emitX64PreemptGiveBack(asm X64RuntimeAsm, machineSlot StackSize)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(machineSlot), wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: PreemptClaimFree)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: MOffSignalPending as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rax)
end 'emitX64PreemptGiveBack'

// `__gt_preempt_self_register(rcx = m)` — see `SchedRuntime.PreemptSelfRegisterName`. Run by the thread that
// is becoming the machine, because neither fact is knowable anywhere else: `GetCurrentThread` is a pseudo-
// handle that names "whoever asks", and the joinable handle the CREATOR got back is 0 for the main OS thread.
//
// ⚠ **A REFUSED `OpenThread` LEAVES THE HANDLE 0 AND THE MACHINE KEEPS THE COOPERATIVE PATH.** `__gt_preempt_m`
// tests for it and declines; a machine that cannot be stopped still yields at every prologue, which is what
// every lane without this mechanism does.
function buildPreemptSelfRegisterChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotMachine = 0
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	// `GetCurrentThreadId()` answers a DWORD; the zero-extending `mov eax, eax` is what makes the id a whole
	// word before it is stored and handed on.
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetCurrentThreadId))
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: false)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: MOffOsThreadId as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// `OpenThread(PreemptThreadAccess, FALSE, id)`
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: PreemptThreadAccess)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: PreemptWin32False)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.OpenThread))

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: MOffPreemptThreadHandle as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	return asm.finishFunction(PreemptSelfRegisterName)
end 'buildPreemptSelfRegisterChunk'

// `__gt_preempt_m(rcx = m)` — see `SchedRuntime.PreemptMachineName`. Go's `preemptM` (`signal_windows.go`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ORDER OF THE TWO CLAIMS IS THE WHOLE PROTOCOL.** The machine's `signalPending` goes up FIRST, so
// at most one stop is ever in flight against one machine and the trampoline's clear is what re-arms it; then
// the process-wide `preemptExtLock`, which `__gt_exit_process` takes and never releases — a thread suspended
// inside `ExitProcess` holds the loader lock the terminating thread needs, and the process hangs with nothing
// printed anywhere.
//
// ⛔ **EVERY ROAD THAT DOES NOT INJECT MUST HAND `signalPending` BACK**, or that machine is never preempted
// again for the life of the process. Only the road that reaches `SetThreadContext` successfully leaves it
// raised, because only then is a trampoline going to run and clear it.
//
// ⚠ **THE IMPORT CALLS ARE DIRECT, NOT SHIMMED.** `__sysmon` is this chunk's only caller and it is not a
// machine (`SchedRuntime.buildSysmon`), so it runs on a real OS-thread stack with no green thread to switch
// off and no processor to hand away.
function buildPreemptMachineChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotMachine = 0
	let SlotHandle = 1
	let SlotContext = 2
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(3)

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	emitX64PreemptClaim(asm, m: X64Register.rcx, alreadyOutstanding: PreemptDoneLabel)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rcx, disp: MOffPreemptThreadHandle as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandle), srcReg: X64Register.r10)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.r10, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptGiveBackLabel)

	// CAS(preemptExtLock, free -> held). A loss means a thread of this process is exiting it.
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.r10, label: SchedPreemptExtLockLabel)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: PreemptClaimFree)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: PreemptClaimHeld)
	encodeLockedRmwBaseDispReg(asm.code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeCmpxchg2ndByte, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0, reg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: PreemptGiveBackLabel)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.SuspendThread))
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: false)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: SuspendThreadFailed)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptUnlockLabel)

	// The thread IS suspended from here on, so every road out goes through `PreemptResumeLabel`.
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.r10, label: SchedPreemptContextLabel)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotContext), srcReg: X64Register.r10)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: Win64ContextControl)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: Win64ContextFlagsOffset as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.r10, wide: true)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetThreadContext))
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: false)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: PreemptWin32False)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptResumeLabel)

	// `__gt_preempt_safe(m, pc, sp)` — the third argument rides RAX, which is this compiler's own third
	// argument register (`StdToX64Conversion.argRegisters`) and not Win64's.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotContext), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.r10, disp: Win64ContextRipOffset as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: argRegisterAt(2), base: X64Register.r10, disp: Win64ContextRspOffset as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: PreemptSafeName)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.r8, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptResumeLabel)

	emitX64PreemptInject(asm, fileSlot: SlotContext, spOffset: Win64ContextRspOffset as Imm32, pcOffset: Win64ContextRipOffset as Imm32)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.r10, wide: true)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.SetThreadContext))
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: false)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: PreemptWin32False)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptResumeLabel)

	// The stop has landed: resume the thread and release the window — and leave `signalPending` raised,
	// because the trampoline that is about to run is what clears it.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ResumeThread))
	emitPreemptReleaseExtLock(asm)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: PreemptDoneLabel)

	// resume: suspended, not injected.
	asm.placeLabel(PreemptResumeLabel)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ResumeThread))

	// unlock: the window is held, nothing is suspended.
	asm.placeLabel(PreemptUnlockLabel)
	emitPreemptReleaseExtLock(asm)

	// giveBack: nothing is held but this machine's `signalPending`.
	asm.placeLabel(PreemptGiveBackLabel)
	emitX64PreemptGiveBack(asm, machineSlot: SlotMachine)

	asm.placeLabel(PreemptDoneLabel)
	return asm.finishFunction(PreemptMachineName)
end 'buildPreemptMachineChunk'

// Release `__sched_preempt_ext_lock`. A plain store, because x86-64 is total store order: every write this
// window made is already visible to any thread that then wins the claim.
function emitPreemptReleaseExtLock(asm X64RuntimeAsm)
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.r10, label: SchedPreemptExtLockLabel)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: PreemptClaimFree)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0, srcReg: X64Register.rax)
end 'emitPreemptReleaseExtLock'

// `__gt_async_preempt` — see `SchedRuntime.AsyncPreemptName`. NOT a normal call: it is entered by a `ret`
// the monitor manufactured, with the interrupted PC where a return address would be and every register and
// flag still holding the interrupted function's values.
//
// ⛔ **THE FLAGS ARE SAVED BEFORE THE STACK IS ALIGNED**, because `and rsp, -16` writes them. They are
// restored last for the same reason — nothing between the reload of the last register and `popfq` touches
// them.
//
// ⚠ **EIGHT BYTES PER XMM, NOT SIXTEEN, AND THAT IS THIS COMPILER'S OWN INVARIANT rather than a shortcut.**
// Every XMM value the backend produces is a scalar f64 and nothing reads an upper half —
// `encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp`'s header states it and the context switch already preserves callee-saved XMMs
// through 8-byte slots.
//
// It runs on the green thread's own stack, so what it puts there is certified against the margin the
// safe-point predicate demanded (`PreemptionDepth.requireAsyncPreemptionFitsItsMargin`).
function buildGtAsyncPreemptChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	assertAsyncPreemptGprOrder()
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()

	_ = emitAsyncPreemptBody(code, callFixups: callFixups)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(AsyncPreemptName, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(), iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(), rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildGtAsyncPreemptChunk'

// The trampoline's bytes, and the answer `x64GtAsyncPreemptStackBytes` measures itself with — one emitter, so
// the certificate cannot describe a frame the chunk does not lay.
function emitAsyncPreemptBody(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray) returns StackSize
	let blockBytes = asyncPreemptBlockBytes()

	encodePushReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rbp, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	code.push(OpcodePushfq)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAnd, reg: X64Register.rsp, imm: (0 - StackFrameAlignment) as Imm32)
	encodePrologue(code, stackSize: blockBytes, probeScratchIsFree: false)

	emitAsyncPreemptRegisterBlock(code, direction: GtRegisterBlockDirection.save)
	encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: AsyncPreemptParkName)
	emitAsyncPreemptRegisterBlock(code, direction: GtRegisterBlockDirection.restore)

	// `lea` writes no flags, so the frame comes home with the interrupted flags still on the stack below it.
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rsp, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: -(StackWordBytes as Imm32))
	code.push(OpcodePopfq)
	encodePopReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
	code.push(OpcodeRet)

	// The pushed rbp and flags, the most the alignment can drop from a word-aligned rsp, and the block.
	return ((2 * StackWordBytes) + (StackFrameAlignment - StackWordBytes)) as StackSize + blockBytes
end 'emitAsyncPreemptBody'

// Every general register and every XMM, one 8-byte slot each above the shadow space the park may clobber.
function emitAsyncPreemptRegisterBlock(code ByteArray, direction GtRegisterBlockDirection)
	for (iter, reg) in AsyncPreemptGprOrder.withIterator() 'gprs'
		let disp = (ShadowSpaceBytes + ((iter.index() as StackSize) * StackWordBytes)) as Imm32

		match direction 'dir'
			save then encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: disp, srcReg: reg)
			restore then encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: reg, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: disp, wide: true)
		end 'dir'
	end 'gprs'

	for (iter, reg) in asyncPreemptXmmOrder().withIterator() 'xmms'
		let disp = (ShadowSpaceBytes + asyncPreemptGprBytes() + ((iter.index() as StackSize) * XmmSaveSlotBytes)) as Imm32

		match direction 'dir'
			save then encodeMovsdBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: disp, srcReg: reg)
			restore then encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: reg, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: disp)
		end 'dir'
	end 'xmms'
end 'emitAsyncPreemptRegisterBlock'

// ⭐ **EVERY XMM, DERIVED FROM THE TWO LISTS THAT ALREADY PARTITION THEM** — the FP argument registers (the
// caller-saved half) and the callee-saved half — rather than a third list of sixteen names that could drift
// from either. The count is asserted because the partition is what makes the derivation total.
function asyncPreemptXmmOrder() returns X64RegisterArray
	var all = X64RegisterArray.create()

	for reg in argFpRegisters 'callerSaved'
		all.push(reg)
	end 'callerSaved'

	for reg in calleeSavedXmmOrder 'calleeSaved'
		all.push(reg)
	end 'calleeSaved'

	if all.count() != vectorRegisterCount(x64RegisterFile()) 'notTheWholeFile'
		panic("asyncPreemptXmmOrder: the caller-saved and callee-saved XMM lists name {all.count()} registers between them, not the {vectorRegisterCount(x64RegisterFile())} the file has — a stop lands at an arbitrary instruction, so a register missing from this block is one the interrupted function loses")
	end 'notTheWholeFile'

	return all
end 'asyncPreemptXmmOrder'

// The GPR half of the block, which is also where the XMM half starts.
function asyncPreemptGprBytes() returns StackSize
	return (AsyncPreemptGprOrder.count() * StackWordBytes) as StackSize
end 'asyncPreemptGprBytes'

// The whole block: the shadow space the park's own callees may clobber, then a slot per register, rounded so
// rsp is 16-aligned at the `call`.
function asyncPreemptBlockBytes() returns StackSize
	return alignUpStack(ShadowSpaceBytes + asyncPreemptGprBytes() + ((vectorRegisterCount(x64RegisterFile()) * XmmSaveSlotBytes) as StackSize), alignment: StackFrameAlignment)
end 'asyncPreemptBlockBytes'

// ⭐ **WHAT AN ASYNCHRONOUS STOP COSTS THE GREEN THREAD's STACK BELOW THE INTERRUPTED RSP, BEFORE
// `__gt_async_preempt_park` RUNS**: the injected return address, the trampoline's frame read off its own
// emitter, and the `call` that enters the park.
export function x64GtAsyncPreemptStackBytes() returns StackSize
	return (2 * StackWordBytes) as StackSize + emitAsyncPreemptBody(ByteArray.create(), callFixups: CallFixupArray.create())
end 'x64GtAsyncPreemptStackBytes'

// `__gt_exit_process(rcx = exitCode)` — see `SchedRuntime.ExitProcessGateName`. Go's `osPreemptExtEnter`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT TAKES `__sched_preempt_ext_lock` AND NEVER GIVES IT BACK, WHICH IS THE POINT.** From here on the
// monitor's compare-and-swap fails and no thread of this process is suspended again — so no thread can be
// suspended INSIDE `ExitProcess`, holding the loader lock this one is about to need. The spin is bounded by
// one suspend/inject/resume window, which is a handful of kernel calls.
//
// ⚠ **A THREAD SUSPENDED WHILE SPINNING HERE IS FINE**: its PC is inside this chunk, which claims no
// `__symtable` preemptible flag, so the safe-point predicate declines it and the monitor resumes it.
function buildExitProcessGateChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)
	let SlotExitCode = 0

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotExitCode), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	encodeLeaRegGlobal(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: X64Register.r10, label: SchedPreemptExtLockLabel)

	asm.placeLabel(ExitProcessSpinLabel)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: PreemptClaimFree)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: PreemptClaimHeld)
	encodeLockedRmwBaseDispReg(asm.code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeCmpxchg2ndByte, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0, reg: X64Register.rdx)
	asm.code.push(OpcodePauseRepPrefix)
	asm.code.push(OpcodePauseNop)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: ExitProcessSpinLabel)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotExitCode), wide: true)
	emitGtGatedIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, callFixups: asm.callFixups, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ExitProcess), usesGt: true)

	// `ExitProcess` never returns; the closer is what makes this a well-formed chunk.
	return asm.finishFunction(ExitProcessGateName)
end 'buildExitProcessGateChunk'

/// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE SIGNALLING LANE'S THREE (x64-linux)
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SAME PROTOCOL AS THE SUSPENDING LANE, WITH THE REGISTER FILE READ BY THE THREAD ITSELF.** The
// monitor cannot read another thread's registers on POSIX, so it sends a signal and the kernel saves the
// file into a `ucontext` the handler may rewrite — Go's `preemptM` and `doSigPreempt`. Everything above the
// delivery is shared: the poison still authorises the stop, `__gt_preempt_safe` still judges it, and the
// same `__gt_async_preempt` is what the thread resumes into.
//
// ⛔ **NO `preemptExtLock` AND NO EXIT GATE HERE.** Both exist on the suspending lane because a thread
// suspended inside `ExitProcess` holds the loader lock the terminating thread needs. Nothing is suspended
// here: a signal handler runs to completion on the signalled thread and blocks no other.

// `tgkill(tgid, tid, sig)` — the one delivery that names a THREAD. `kill` names a process and lets the
// kernel pick any thread with the signal unblocked, which is every machine.
let X64LinuxSyscallTgkill = 234

// `__gt_preempt_self_register(rcx = m)` on the signalling lane — see `SchedRuntime.PreemptSelfRegisterName`.
// The thread id is the whole of it: `MOffPreemptThreadHandle` stays 0 because a signal is addressed to the
// id, and this thread's alternate signal stack is installed by `mrt_runtime_init` (the main thread's) or by
// `SchedRuntime.emitSchedWorkerBirth` (a worker's), which is the same stack the fault handler needs.
//
// ⚠ **THE SYSCALL CLOBBERS rcx**, which is the argument register, so the machine goes to a frame slot first.
function buildLinuxPreemptSelfRegisterChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotMachine = 0
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeX64Syscall(asm.code, number: X64LinuxSyscallGettid)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: MOffOsThreadId as Imm32, srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	return asm.finishFunction(PreemptSelfRegisterName)
end 'buildLinuxPreemptSelfRegisterChunk'

// `__gt_preempt_m(rcx = m)` on the signalling lane — Go's `preemptM` (`signal_unix.go`).
//
// ⛔ **EVERY ROAD THAT DOES NOT DELIVER MUST HAND `signalPending` BACK**, or that machine is never preempted
// again for the life of the process. Only a `tgkill` the kernel accepted leaves it raised, because only then
// is a handler going to run — and the handler either injects and lets the trampoline clear it, or declines
// and clears it itself.
//
// ⚠ **A REFUSED `tgkill` IS A THREAD THAT IS GONE**, which is the one failure this can meet: the id was
// recorded by the thread itself and an M is never freed.
function buildLinuxPreemptMachineChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotMachine = 0
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	emitX64PreemptClaim(asm, m: X64Register.rcx, alreadyOutstanding: PreemptDoneLabel)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rcx, disp: MOffOsThreadId as Imm32, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.r10, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptGiveBackLabel)

	// tgkill(getpid(), tid, SIGURG). `syscall` clobbers rcx and r11 and nothing else, so the tid survives the
	// pid read in r10.
	encodeX64Syscall(asm.code, number: X64LinuxSyscallGetPid)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdi, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsi, src: X64Register.r10, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: LinuxSignalUrg)
	encodeX64Syscall(asm.code, number: X64LinuxSyscallTgkill)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.greaterEqual, targetBlockId: PreemptDoneLabel)

	asm.placeLabel(PreemptGiveBackLabel)
	emitX64PreemptGiveBack(asm, machineSlot: SlotMachine)

	asm.placeLabel(PreemptDoneLabel)
	return asm.finishFunction(PreemptMachineName)
end 'buildLinuxPreemptMachineChunk'

// `mrt_preempt_handler(rdi = sig, rsi = info, rdx = ucontext)` — see `SchedRuntime.PreemptHandlerName`. The
// kernel's `SA_SIGINFO` entry ABI, as `mrt_fault_handler`'s is.
//
// ⛔ **IT RETURNS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE INJECTION WORK AT ALL.** The rewrite it makes to the SAVED
// register file is applied by `rt_sigreturn` on the way out; a handler that exited would rewrite nothing.
//
// ⛔⛔ **EVERY ROAD HANDS `MOffSignalPending` BACK, AND THE ONE THAT CANNOT NAME A MACHINE HAS NO CLAIM TO
// HAND BACK.** A machine left claimed is one the monitor never stops again for the life of the process. The
// sender aims `tgkill` at `MOffOsThreadId`, which `emitSchedBecomeM` records AFTER publishing this thread's
// TLS slot — so a thread this runtime can signal always has a slot, and a handler that finds none is
// answering a SIGURG this runtime did not send. It returns without touching a word it has no owner for.
//
// Slots: 0 = ucontext, 1 = m.
function buildLinuxPreemptHandlerChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotUcontext = 0
	let SlotMachine = 1
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(2)

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotUcontext), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)

	emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, dest: X64Register.rax, bail: PreemptNoMachineLabel, os: Os.linux)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// `__gt_preempt_safe(m, pc, sp)` over the register file the kernel saved. The third argument rides RAX,
	// this compiler's own third argument register (`StdToX64Conversion.argRegisters`) — which is why the
	// machine is re-read from its slot rather than kept in the register it arrived in.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r10, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotUcontext), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.r10, disp: X64LinuxUContextRipOffset, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: argRegisterAt(2), base: X64Register.r10, disp: X64LinuxUContextRspOffset, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMachine), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: PreemptSafeName)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: X64Register.r8, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: PreemptGiveBackLabel)

	// The injection, written into the saved file rather than into live registers — the same act the Windows
	// monitor performs on a `CONTEXT`, through the one emitter both come to.
	emitX64PreemptInject(asm, fileSlot: SlotUcontext, spOffset: X64LinuxUContextRspOffset, pcOffset: X64LinuxUContextRipOffset)

	// The stop has landed: `signalPending` stays raised for the trampoline to clear.
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: PreemptDoneLabel)

	// Declined: this instant is no safe point, so the machine is re-armed for the monitor's next poll.
	asm.placeLabel(PreemptGiveBackLabel)
	emitX64PreemptGiveBack(asm, machineSlot: SlotMachine)

	// A SIGURG this runtime did not send — see the header. There is no claim of ours to hand back.
	asm.placeLabel(PreemptNoMachineLabel)

	asm.placeLabel(PreemptDoneLabel)
	return asm.finishFunction(PreemptHandlerName)
end 'buildLinuxPreemptHandlerChunk'

// Append the hand-assembled GT chunks — called by BackendDispatch when the module spawns a green thread, alongside
// `appendX64RuntimeChunks` for the panic runtime. They lay out above user code like every other runtime chunk, so
// `mrt_start` stays at `.text` offset 0. `usesIocp` (P1.5 slice 1a-ii) additionally appends the IOCP completion-drain
// thread entry.
export function appendX64GtRuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, usesIocp bool, target Target)
	chunks.push(buildGtContextSwitchChunk(target))
	chunks.push(buildGtTrampolineChunk())
	chunks.push(buildGtGuardTrippedChunk())
	chunks.push(buildGtMorestackChunk(target))
	chunks.push(buildGtStackRelocateChunk(target))

	// ⛔ **GATE SITE — the import shim's own door.** It is the shim's, not asynchronous preemption's: the
	// shim calls it unconditionally and only a lane whose kernel calls go through an IAT emits a shim at all.
	if x64OsCallKind(target.os) == X64OsCallKind.importCall 'theImportShim'
		chunks.push(buildGtShimEnterChunk(target))
	end 'theImportShim'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE COMPLETION-DRAIN THREAD IS THE ONE CHUNK HERE THAT IS NOT MERELY OS-PARAMETRIC BUT OS-ONLY,
	// AND ITS GATE IS `ioCompletionShape` RATHER THAN THE OS.** `usesIocp` asks *"does this module read a
	// pipe?"*, which is true on every lane; whether a read COMPLETES SOMEWHERE ELSE is the target's question,
	// and only Windows answers `completionPort`. This is the same pairing `installGtRuntime` makes before it
	// builds the port's Std-tier half, so the thread and the port it drains ride one fact.
	if usesIocp and ioCompletionShape(target) == IoCompletionShape.completionPort 'io'
		chunks.push(buildIoCompletionLoopChunk())
	end 'io'

	// ⛔ **GATE SITE — asynchronous preemption.** The trampoline is ISA-shaped and is the same bytes whichever
	// way the stop was delivered; what a machine records about its own thread, and how the monitor reaches
	// that thread, are the OS's. See `SchedRuntime.targetPreemptDelivery`.
	if targetStopsThreadsForPreemption(target) 'itCanStopAThread'
		chunks.push(buildGtAsyncPreemptChunk())

		match targetPreemptDelivery(target) 'delivery'
			threadSuspend then appendX64SuspendPreemptChunks(chunks)
			signal then appendX64SignalPreemptChunks(chunks)
			none then panic("appendX64GtRuntimeChunks: `targetStopsThreadsForPreemption` admitted {targetToString(target)}, which `targetPreemptDelivery` says has no delivery mechanism at all — the two have drifted")
		end 'delivery'
	end 'itCanStopAThread'
end 'appendX64GtRuntimeChunks'

// x64-windows: the monitor reads and writes the stopped thread's register file, and every exit closes its
// window first (`SchedRuntime.ExitProcessGateName`).
function appendX64SuspendPreemptChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray)
	chunks.push(buildPreemptSelfRegisterChunk())
	chunks.push(buildPreemptMachineChunk())
	chunks.push(buildExitProcessGateChunk())
end 'appendX64SuspendPreemptChunks'

// x64-linux: the thread reads its own file inside a signal handler, so the third chunk is that handler
// rather than an exit gate.
function appendX64SignalPreemptChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray)
	chunks.push(buildLinuxPreemptSelfRegisterChunk())
	chunks.push(buildLinuxPreemptMachineChunk())
	chunks.push(buildLinuxPreemptHandlerChunk())
end 'appendX64SignalPreemptChunks'
